CClib.library/--background--                      CClib.library/--background--

    CClib.library is a shared AmigaOS implementation of the standard C
    library (plus Amiga helpers). Applications OpenLibrary("CClib.library",
    4), include the CClib SDK headers (<stdio.h>, <string.h>, ...) instead
    of the SAS/C clib, and either include <proto/cclib.h> (register LVOs)
    or link the auto/stub library for a normal C calling face.

    Per-task state lives in Task->tc_UserData (task_UserData). Call
    SetupSTDIO from CRT before using stdio/heap; ClearSTDIO on exit.

    Implementation uses native AmigaOS APIs (exec/dos/utility) only -
    no dependency on the SAS/C sc.lib runtime.

    WARNING
	Do not overwrite tc_UserData while CClib is active.
	printf/scanf LVOs take a format string and an APTR argument array
	(RawDoFmt style). C varargs "..." belongs in the stub/auto layer
	or client wrappers, not in the raw LVO register signature.
	Opaque types (FILE *, task_UserData *, struct tm *, ...) are APTR at
	the LVO boundary; client headers restore typed C declarations.
	%e/%f/%g consume a DOUBLE as two consecutive LONGs in the arg array.

    REENTRANCY (historic CClib.Doc)
	Mutable C "globals" live in per-task task_UserData (heap freelist,
	FILE/fd lists, strtok scanpoint, tmpnam/asctime/localtime buffers,
	children, errno/blocksize/mathbase pointers).  Tasks never share a
	freelist.  The ctype table in the library is shared and read-only.
	Classic static-buffer APIs are per-task safe, not call-safe within
	one task.  FILEs are linked lists - use fileno(), not hard-coded 0/1/2.

    FLOATING POINT (historic CClib.Doc)
	Library FP uses only mathieeedoubbas.library (64-bit IEEE).  CRT opens
	it when _math != 0 and passes the base to SetupSTDIO as mathbase.
	atof and printf/scanf %e/%f/%g call IEEEDP* through that base - never
	SAS/C softfloat.  Apps that use float must compile/link IEEE (historic
	-fi + lcmieee.lib; SAS/C scmieee / mathieee*).  Set _math = 0 to skip
	opening IEEE when the app needs no float.

    SEE ALSO
	libraries/cclib.h, proto/cclib.h, SDK/SFD/cclib_lib.sfd,
	CClib.library/--notes-sasc--

CClib.library/--notes-sasc--                      CClib.library/--notes-sasc--
   NAME
	Notes on SAS/C (Lattice C 6.x)

   FUNCTION
	SAS/C is the Lattice C 6 line.  Historic CClib.Doc section IX (Lattice)
	still applies; this port maps it onto SAS/C 6.x / SLINK as follows.

	Historic Lattice link order
	    cc.o + lcmain.o + yourstuff.o ... lcclib.lib, lcmieee.lib,
	    lc.lib, amiga.lib

	    cc.o was a patched Lattice c.o (source not redistributable).
	    Lines that prepended the program name to the CLI string and that
	    replied to the Workbench startup message were removed, because
	    CClib.library already does both.  Symptoms of using stock c.o:
		CLI - argv[0] and argv[1] both set to the program name
		Workbench - double ReplyMsg, system crash

	This port (NOSTARTUP)
	    Source/crt/cc_entry.o  ~=  cc.o   (SysBase/DOSBase, GetArgStr,
	                                     no argv[0] prepend, no WB reply)
	    Source/crt/cc_crt.o    ~=  lcmain.o  (_main(aptr), SetupSTDIO,
	                                     __fpinit, __XCEXIT/Exit,
	                                     Aztec _savsp/_stkbase/_oldtrap)
	    stubs/math.o, stubs/blocksize.o
	    your app .o
	    LIB lib:amiga.lib only - do not link sc:lib/c.o or sc.lib

	    SLINK: NOSTARTUP ENTRY=_start
	    See Source/unittests/smakefile (heir of archive test.lmk).

	If WBenchMsg is already non-NULL when _main runs, stock c.o was
	linked; _main exits 100 (same guard as lcmain.c).

	Floating point
	    CRT: OpenLibrary("mathieeedoubbas.library") when _math != 0;
	    pass base to SetupSTDIO.  Library: Source/cc_ieee.c IEEEDP*
	    wrappers using ud->mathbase (atof, %e/%f/%g).  No sc.lib
	    softfloat in the .library.  SLINK Warning 625 when omitting
	    scm*.lib on the library link is advisory.

	Headers
	    Include CClib SDK headers (Include_H) ahead of SAS/C clib.
	    Define ANSIC (or rely on the SDK headers' default) for prototypes.
	    Application LVOs: <proto/cclib.h> (register) or the auto/stub lib
	    for C varargs/stack calling.

   SEE ALSO
	archive CClib.Doc (intro reentrancy/IEEE, section IX), Source/crt/,
	Source/cc_ieee.c, Source/unittests/, proto/cclib.h

CClib.library/SetSTDIO                                  CClib.library/SetSTDIO
   NAME
	SetSTDIO -- allocate per-task C library userdata

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG SetSTDIO(void);

   FUNCTION
	Allocates a task_UserData structure from the public memory pool and attaches it to the current task's tc_UserData field. The block holds per-task FILE and FileDesc lists, heap freelist anchors, argv parsing results, temporary name buffers, and pointers into the application's errno, blocksize, and stdio globals once SetupSTDIO runs.

	The previous tc_UserData pointer is saved in old_ud so ClearSTDIO can restore it. Lists for RAM allocations, open file descriptors, stream FILE nodes, and child processes are initialized empty. Call SetSTDIO (or let SetupSTDIO call it) before any other CClib routine that touches task state.

	Do not overwrite tc_UserData while CClib is active. Only one task_UserData block should be attached per task at a time.

   INPUTS
	None.

   RESULT
	LONG status. Zero is returned if the allocation and attach succeeded. One is returned on failure (no current task or AllocMem failed). No errno is set.

   NOTES
	task_UserData lives at Task->tc_UserData. GetSTDIO returns the same pointer. SetupSTDIO may call SetSTDIO implicitly when tc_UserData is still NULL.

   SEE ALSO
	GetSTDIO, ClearSTDIO, SetupSTDIO, closeall

   (LVO bias -30)

CClib.library/GetSTDIO                                  CClib.library/GetSTDIO
   NAME
	GetSTDIO -- return the current task's userdata pointer

   SYNOPSIS
	APTR GetSTDIO(void);

   FUNCTION
	Returns a pointer to the cc_task_UserData block currently attached to the calling task. Nearly every CClib LVO begins by fetching this block to reach open-file lists, stream buffers, errno storage, and heap state.

	The call is implemented as a compact assembly helper because it is invoked frequently from library internals. Client code may use it to inspect argc/argv after SetupSTDIO or to reach library-owned fields documented in libraries/cclib.h.

   INPUTS
	None.

   RESULT
	APTR to task_UserData on success, or NULL if SetSTDIO has not been run for this task (tc_UserData is not a CClib block).

   NOTES
	Opaque at the LVO boundary (APTR); client headers typedef it as task_UserData *. Never replace tc_UserData manually while CClib is active.

   SEE ALSO
	SetSTDIO, ClearSTDIO, SetupSTDIO

   (LVO bias -36)

CClib.library/ClearSTDIO                              CClib.library/ClearSTDIO
   NAME
	ClearSTDIO -- tear down CClib per-task state

   SYNOPSIS
	void ClearSTDIO(void);

   FUNCTION
	Ends the CClib process session started by SetupSTDIO (or SetSTDIO).
	Tear-down order: scdir_clean, kill_children, free getenv scratch,
	DeleteMsgPort(exeport) if present, closeall, freeall, restore
	tc_UserData from old_ud, FreeMem the task_UserData block.

	CRT exit/abort paths call ClearSTDIO after atexit handlers. Use
	exit/_exit (not AmigaDOS Exit alone) so the CRT can reach this.

   INPUTS
	None.

   RESULT
	None.

   NOTES
	Unlike closeall alone, ClearSTDIO also frees heap memory, child
	process state, and restores tc_UserData. Future rename InitCCLib /
	TermCCLib may alias SetupSTDIO / ClearSTDIO without changing this
	session contract.

   SEE ALSO
	SetSTDIO, GetSTDIO, closeall, SetupSTDIO, freeall

   (LVO bias -42)

CClib.library/closeall                                  CClib.library/closeall
   NAME
	closeall -- close every open stream and file descriptor

   SYNOPSIS
	void closeall(void);

   FUNCTION
	Walks StreamFiles then OpenFiles in task_UserData. For each FILE:
	fflush if dirty, DeleteFile tmpfile paths (CC_TEMP), free library
	buffers (CC_ALLBUF). For each FileDesc: Close BPTR unless CC_O_STDIO.
	Clears application stdin/stdout/stderr pointers and frees list nodes.

   INPUTS
	None.

   RESULT
	None.

   NOTES
	closeall does not free the task_UserData block or heap RAM from
	malloc/calloc. For full session teardown use ClearSTDIO.

   SEE ALSO
	ClearSTDIO, fclose, close, SetupSTDIO

   (LVO bias -48)

CClib.library/SetAbortFunc                          CClib.library/SetAbortFunc
   NAME
	SetAbortFunc -- install a Ctrl-C / break handler

   SYNOPSIS
	void SetAbortFunc(APTR abortfunc);

	Registers: a0=abortfunc

   FUNCTION
	Registers abort_func in task_UserData. When AmigaDOS delivers a break signal (console Ctrl-C or the Break command), the library checks this pointer and, if non-NULL, calls func(signalmask) with the 32-bit signal set that caused the break.

	The default handler installed by SetupSTDIO is the application's exit routine (typically _exit). Replace it when you need to unwind custom state before terminating.

	If you intend to leave the program from the handler, call exit or _exit, not AmigaDOS Exit(), or CClib allocations and open files will not be released.

   INPUTS
	abortfunc - APTR to a function void func(LONG signalmask), or NULL to clear the hook (same effect as ClearAbortFunc).

   RESULT
	None.

   NOTES
	SetupSTDIO also stores its exitfunc argument in abort_func. A later SetAbortFunc overrides that pointer until ClearAbortFunc runs.

   SEE ALSO
	ClearAbortFunc, SetupSTDIO, ClearSTDIO, signal_child

   (LVO bias -54)

CClib.library/ClearAbortFunc                      CClib.library/ClearAbortFunc
   NAME
	ClearAbortFunc -- remove the break signal handler

   SYNOPSIS
	void ClearAbortFunc(void);

   FUNCTION
	Sets abort_func in task_UserData to NULL so break signals are no longer forwarded to application code. The library does not install a replacement; the next SetupSTDIO call will set abort_func again from its exitfunc argument.

	Use this when a subsystem that registered a temporary handler has finished and you want breaks ignored until reconfigured.

   INPUTS
	None.

   RESULT
	None.

   NOTES
	Break handling still depends on process signal masks set elsewhere; clearing the func only removes the C callback.

   SEE ALSO
	SetAbortFunc, SetupSTDIO

   (LVO bias -60)

CClib.library/SetupSTDIO                              CClib.library/SetupSTDIO
   NAME
	SetupSTDIO -- wire globals, parse args, open standard streams

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG SetupSTDIO(APTR stdin_p, APTR stdout_p, APTR stderr_p,
	APTR errno_p, APTR blocksize_p, APTR type_p, APTR mathbase,
	LONG alen, STRPTR aptr, APTR wbm, APTR exitfunc);

	Registers: a0=stdin_p, a1=stdout_p, a2=stderr_p, a3=errno_p,
		a4=blocksize_p, a5=type_p, d0=mathbase, d1=alen,
		d2=aptr, d3=wbm, d4=exitfunc

   FUNCTION
	Begins the CClib process session (CRT calls this before main). Connects CClib to the application's global variables and prepares standard I/O for the task. If tc_UserData is not yet set, SetupSTDIO calls SetSTDIO first. It then records pointers to the application's stdin, stdout, stderr FILE pointers, errno, blocksize, and (optionally) ctype table pointer, and stores mathbase for IEEE double helpers.

	Command-line programs pass alen and aptr; SetupSTDIO calls cli_parse to build argc/argv from the CLI argument string and command name. Workbench programs pass wbm (struct WBStartup *); wb_parse builds argv from the tool's argument list and, when the icon has a WINDOW tooltype, opens a CON: (or similar) window for standard I/O.

	When standard I/O is available, Input(), Output(), and ErrorOutput() are wrapped in FILE streams and FileDesc records on the task lists. CLI streams are tagged CC_O_STDIO so close() will not Close() the underlying DOS handles.

   INPUTS
	stdin_p - address of the application's FILE * stdin variable
	stdout_p - address of FILE * stdout
	stderr_p - address of FILE * stderr
	errno_p - address of the application's errno (LONG)
	blocksize_p - address of blocksize used by the heap manager
	type_p - address of char *; receives pointer to library ctype table
	mathbase - MathIeeeDoubBasBase pointer, or NULL if _math == 0
	alen - CLI argument string length (0 if wbm is used)
	aptr - CLI argument string pointer
	wbm - Workbench startup message, or NULL for CLI
	exitfunc - application exit routine (stored as abort_func)

   RESULT
	LONG status. One is returned when initialization succeeded (including the case where WB has no WINDOW tooltype and stdio is deliberately absent). Zero is returned if SetSTDIO could not allocate task_UserData.

   NOTES
	Workbench tools without a WINDOW tooltype get argc/argv but no stdin/stdout/stderr FILEs; writing to NULL streams can crash. printf, fprintf, fscanf, and scanf LVOs take a format string plus an APTR argument array (RawDoFmt style); varargs belong in stub/auto layers. %e/%f/%g consume a DOUBLE as two consecutive LONGs in that array when mathbase is non-NULL.

   SEE ALSO
	SetSTDIO, GetSTDIO, ClearSTDIO, cli_parse, wb_parse, atof

   (LVO bias -66)

CClib.library/strerror                                  CClib.library/strerror
   NAME
	strerror -- map an errno code to a text message

   SYNOPSIS
	STRPTR strerror(LONG err);

	Registers: d0=err

   FUNCTION
	Returns a pointer to a static English description for the small errno set used by CClib (CC_E* in cc_internal.h). Index 0 is "No Error"; codes 1 through 10 cover the common file, memory, and math failures the library sets through cc_set_errno().

	Unknown codes receive the string "Unknown Error". The returned pointer is to library static storage and must not be freed or overwritten.

   INPUTS
	err - errno value (typically from cc_get_errno or *errno_p)

   RESULT
	STRPTR message string. Always non-NULL.

   NOTES
	perror combines the caller's prefix with strerror of the current errno. This is not a complete POSIX strerror table.

   SEE ALSO
	perror, SetupSTDIO

   (LVO bias -72)

CClib.library/open                                          CClib.library/open
   NAME
	open -- open a file and return a file descriptor

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG open(STRPTR name, ULONG mode, ULONG perms);

	Registers: a0=name, d0=mode, d1=perms

   FUNCTION
	Opens the named file through dos.library and allocates a FileDesc node on the task's OpenFiles list. The value returned is a LONG cast of the FileDesc * pointer, not a small integer index like Unix.

	mode combines O_RDONLY, O_WRONLY, O_RDWR, O_CREAT, O_TRUNC, O_EXCL, and O_APPEND from fcntl.h. O_CREAT opens for read/write when the object is missing; O_TRUNC selects MODE_NEWFILE. O_EXCL with O_CREAT fails if the object already exists. O_APPEND seeks to end after open.

	Use fileno() on a FILE * when you need the same FileDesc token for read() or write().

   INPUTS
	name - path string
	mode - open flags (O_*)
	perms - protection mask (reserved; not applied on Amiga)

   RESULT
	FileDesc * as LONG on success. -1 on failure with errno set (ENOENT, EEXIST, ENOMEM, EINVAL).

   NOTES
	Never assume fd 0, 1, or 2; stdio FILEs link to arbitrary FileDesc nodes. Compare only to -1 and NULL, not to small ints.

   SEE ALSO
	close, read, write, lseek, fopen, fdopen, creat

   (LVO bias -78)

CClib.library/close                                        CClib.library/close
   NAME
	close -- close a file descriptor

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG close(LONG fd);

	Registers: d0=fd

   FUNCTION
	Locates the FileDesc for the given token, closes the underlying DOS BPTR unless the descriptor is marked CC_O_STDIO (CLI Input/Output), removes the node from OpenFiles, and frees the list node memory.

	Any FILE stream still referencing this FileDesc via _unit should be fclose()'d first so buffers are flushed.

   INPUTS
	fd - FileDesc * token returned by open() or fileno()

   RESULT
	0 on success. -1 with errno EBADF if fd is 0, -1, or unknown.

   NOTES
	Closing a stdio-backed CLI handle does not Close() DOS Input/Output; only the FileDesc wrapper is released.

   SEE ALSO
	open, fclose, closeall

   (LVO bias -84)

CClib.library/unlink                                      CClib.library/unlink
   NAME
	unlink -- delete a file by name

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG unlink(STRPTR name);

	Registers: a0=name

   FUNCTION
	Deletes the named file using dos.library DeleteFile(). Directories and assign names follow normal AmigaDOS rules; there is no separate remove() LVO.

   INPUTS
	name - path of the file to delete

   RESULT
	0 on success. -1 with errno ENOENT (or EINVAL if name is NULL).

   NOTES
	Failed deletes do not remove open FileDesc nodes; close those separately.

   SEE ALSO
	open, rename, stat

   (LVO bias -90)

CClib.library/stat                                          CClib.library/stat
   NAME
	stat -- read file metadata into a stat buffer

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG stat(STRPTR name, APTR buf);

	Registers: a0=name, a1=buf

   FUNCTION
	Locks the named object and fills a cc_stat structure at buf with size, protection, and date information from a FileInfoBlock. The layout matches the SDK stat.h used by CClib clients.

	Use stat_() when extended fields are required; stat() fills the portable subset.

   INPUTS
	name - path to examine
	buf - APTR to cc_stat (struct stat in client headers)

   RESULT
	0 on success. -1 with errno ENOENT if the lock fails.

   NOTES
	Times are converted from Amiga DateStamp to seconds since 1978-01-01.

   SEE ALSO
	access, open, stat_

   (LVO bias -96)

CClib.library/rename                                      CClib.library/rename
   NAME
	rename -- rename a file or directory

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG rename(STRPTR oldname, STRPTR newname);

	Registers: a0=oldname, a1=newname

   FUNCTION
	Renames oldname to newname through dos.library Rename(). Both paths must be on the same volume for classic AmigaDOS behavior.

   INPUTS
	oldname - current path
	newname - new path

   RESULT
	0 on success. -1 with errno ENOENT or EINVAL.

   NOTES
	Open FileDesc tokens remain valid across rename of the underlying file.

   SEE ALSO
	unlink, open, stat

   (LVO bias -102)

CClib.library/read                                          CClib.library/read
   NAME
	read -- read bytes from a file descriptor

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG read(LONG fd, APTR buf, LONG len);

	Registers: d0=fd, a0=buf, d1=len

   FUNCTION
	Reads up to len bytes from the DOS handle inside the FileDesc into buf using dos.library Read(). Works on binary or text files; no newline translation is performed at this layer.

	Buffered stdio input ultimately calls read() from agetc() when the stream buffer is empty.

   INPUTS
	fd - FileDesc * token
	buf - destination buffer
	len - maximum byte count

   RESULT
	Number of bytes read (0 at EOF), or -1 with errno EBADF.

   NOTES
	fd must be a FileDesc pointer from open(), not a raw BPTR.

   SEE ALSO
	write, open, close, agetc, fread

   (LVO bias -108)

CClib.library/write                                        CClib.library/write
   NAME
	write -- write bytes to a file descriptor

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG write(LONG fd, APTR buf, LONG len);

	Registers: d0=fd, a0=buf, d1=len

   FUNCTION
	Writes len bytes from buf to the DOS handle in the FileDesc using dos.library Write(). This is the low-level path used by fflush() when pushing stream buffers to disk.

	Partial writes are reported by the returned count; check against len when reliability matters.

   INPUTS
	fd - FileDesc * token
	buf - source data
	len - byte count

   RESULT
	Bytes written, or -1 with errno EBADF.

   NOTES
	Stream fflush maps short writes to EOF return on the FILE API.

   SEE ALSO
	read, open, fflush, aputc, fwrite

   (LVO bias -114)

CClib.library/creat                                        CClib.library/creat
   NAME
	creat -- create or truncate a file for writing

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG creat(STRPTR name, ULONG perms);

	Registers: a0=name, d0=perms

   FUNCTION
	Convenience wrapper equivalent to open(name, O_WRONLY|O_CREAT|O_TRUNC, perms). Creates a new file or truncates an existing one to zero length, then returns a FileDesc * token for write-only access.

   INPUTS
	name - path to create
	perms - protection mask (reserved)

   RESULT
	FileDesc * as LONG, or -1 with errno set like open().

   NOTES
	For read/write creation use open() with O_CREAT and O_RDWR.

   SEE ALSO
	open, close, write

   (LVO bias -120)

CClib.library/lseek                                        CClib.library/lseek
   NAME
	lseek -- set or query file position

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG lseek(LONG fd, LONG offset, ULONG whence);

	Registers: d0=fd, d1=offset, d2=whence

   FUNCTION
	Repositions the DOS file pointer for the given FileDesc. whence is SEEK_SET, SEEK_CUR, or SEEK_END (stdio.h). The new absolute position is returned on success.

	Stream-level fseek() calls fflush() first, then empties the FILE buffer before invoking lseek() on the underlying fd.

   INPUTS
	fd - FileDesc * token
	offset - byte offset (signed)
	whence - SEEK_SET, SEEK_CUR, or SEEK_END

   RESULT
	New file position, or -1 with errno EINVAL/EBADF.

   NOTES
	After fseek on a FILE, ungetc pushback uses an empty buffer.

   SEE ALSO
	fseek, ftell, read, write

   (LVO bias -126)

CClib.library/isatty                                      CClib.library/isatty
   NAME
	isatty -- test whether a descriptor is interactive

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG isatty(LONG fd);

	Registers: d0=fd

   FUNCTION
	Calls dos.library IsInteractive() on the BPTR inside the FileDesc. Used by stdio to decide whether to line-buffer output on newline (aputc flushes when isatty is true).

	Returns false for regular files and true for CON: and other interactive handles.

   INPUTS
	fd - FileDesc * token

   RESULT
	Non-zero if interactive. Zero if not, with errno ENOTTY when the handle is a plain file (errno EBADF for bad fd).

   NOTES
	fileno(stream) supplies fd for stdio streams.

   SEE ALSO
	aputc, open, SetupSTDIO

   (LVO bias -132)

CClib.library/access                                      CClib.library/access
   NAME
	access -- check file existence or protection

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG access(STRPTR name, LONG mode);

	Registers: a0=name, d0=mode

   FUNCTION
	Tests whether name can be accessed in the requested mode. mode 0 checks existence only. Bits 4, 2, and 1 correspond to read, write, and execute permission checks against the file's fib_Protection bits.

	Amiga protection uses inverted FIBF_* flags relative to classic Unix R_OK/W_OK/X_OK tests.

   INPUTS
	name - path to test
	mode - 0 or combination of 4 (read), 2 (write), 1 (execute)

   RESULT
	0 if access allowed. -1 with errno ENOENT, EACCES, ENOMEM, or EINVAL.

   NOTES
	Does not open a FileDesc; use open() when you need a handle.

   SEE ALSO
	stat, open

   (LVO bias -138)

CClib.library/fopen                                        CClib.library/fopen
   NAME
	fopen -- open a buffered FILE stream by path

   SYNOPSIS
	APTR fopen(STRPTR name, STRPTR mode);

	Registers: a0=name, a1=mode

   FUNCTION
	Parses mode and calls open() with the equivalent O_* flags, then attaches a FILE node on StreamFiles via fdopen(). Supported modes are r, w, a, r+, w+, and a+; an optional b suffix (binary) is accepted and ignored on Amiga.

	read opens existing; write creates/truncates; append creates or seeks to end. Plus modes add O_RDWR.

   INPUTS
	name - file path
	mode - C fopen mode string

   RESULT
	APTR (FILE *) stream, or NULL with errno set.

   NOTES
	Streams are linked on task_UserData->StreamFiles. Use fileno() for the underlying FileDesc *.

   SEE ALSO
	fclose, fdopen, freopen, fread, fwrite

   (LVO bias -144)

CClib.library/freopen                                    CClib.library/freopen
   NAME
	freopen -- reuse a FILE stream on a new file

   SYNOPSIS
	APTR freopen(STRPTR name, STRPTR mode, APTR stream);

	Registers: a0=name, a1=mode, a2=stream

   FUNCTION
	Closes the underlying FileDesc for stream (after fflush), frees any library-owned buffer, deletes a temporary backing file when CC_TEMP is set, and opens name with a fresh FileDesc while reusing the same FILE structure.

	Useful for redirecting stdin/stdout or recycling a static FILE allocated by the application.

   INPUTS
	name - new path
	mode - fopen-style mode string
	stream - existing FILE *

   RESULT
	stream on success, or NULL with errno set (stream left closed).

   NOTES
	Temporary files created by tmpfile() store the path in _tmpname.

   SEE ALSO
	fopen, fclose, tmpfile

   (LVO bias -150)

CClib.library/fclose                                      CClib.library/fclose
   NAME
	fclose -- flush and close a FILE stream

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG fclose(APTR stream);

	Registers: a0=stream

   FUNCTION
	Calls fflush() to push dirty output, closes the underlying FileDesc when _unit is valid, frees library-allocated buffer memory, deletes temporary backing files for tmpfile() streams, and removes the FILE node from StreamFiles.

	After fclose the FILE pointer must not be used.

   INPUTS
	stream - FILE * to close

   RESULT
	0 on success. -1 if fflush, close, or validation failed (errno set).

   NOTES
	CLI stdio FILEs close wrappers without Closing DOS Input/Output.

   SEE ALSO
	fopen, fflush, close, closeall

   (LVO bias -156)

CClib.library/fdopen                                      CClib.library/fdopen
   NAME
	fdopen -- wrap an existing file descriptor in a FILE stream

   SYNOPSIS
	APTR fdopen(LONG fd, STRPTR mode);

	Registers: d0=fd, a0=mode

   FUNCTION
	Allocates a new FILE node on StreamFiles and sets _unit to the given FileDesc * token. The mode string is currently unused but should match how the fd was opened.

	Multiple FILE streams may share one FileDesc when stdio duplicates CLI handles (stdin/stdout overlap).

   INPUTS
	fd - FileDesc * from open()
	mode - fopen-style hint (ignored)

   RESULT
	APTR (FILE *) or NULL with errno EINVAL.

   NOTES
	Do not fdopen the same fd twice unless you understand shared _unit lifetime.

   SEE ALSO
	fopen, fileno, close

   (LVO bias -162)

CClib.library/getbuff                                    CClib.library/getbuff
   NAME
	getbuff -- allocate the default stream buffer

   SYNOPSIS
	void getbuff(APTR stream);

	Registers: a0=stream

   FUNCTION
	If stream has no buffer yet, allocates CC_BUFSIZ bytes for _buff or falls back to the single-byte _bytbuf embedded in the FILE when memory is low. Sets _bp and _bend to the start of the buffer and marks CC_ALLBUF when the library owns the allocation.

	Called lazily from agetc and aputc before first buffered I/O.

   INPUTS
	stream - FILE *

   RESULT
	None.

   NOTES
	Use setvbuf() for custom buffering; getbuff() is the automatic default.

   SEE ALSO
	setvbuf, agetc, aputc

   (LVO bias -168)

CClib.library/setvbuf                                    CClib.library/setvbuf
   NAME
	setvbuf -- configure stream buffering

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG setvbuf(APTR stream, STRPTR buf, LONG mode, ULONG size);

	Registers: a0=stream, a1=buf, d0=mode, d1=size

   FUNCTION
	Selects full buffering, line buffering, or no buffering for stream. Calls fflush() before changing state. When buf is non-NULL the caller supplies storage of size bytes. When buf is NULL and mode is not _IONBF, the library AllocMem's size bytes and sets CC_ALLBUF.

	Returns CC_HASBUF if a library-owned buffer already exists and cannot be replaced, or CC_NOBUFMEM if allocation fails.

   INPUTS
	stream - FILE *
	buf - caller buffer or NULL
	mode - _IOFBF, _IOLBF, or _IONBF
	size - buffer length

   RESULT
	0 on success. CC_HASBUF or CC_NOBUFMEM on failure (see stdio.h).

   NOTES
	_IONBF or size 0 uses the one-byte _bytbuf (unbuffered behavior).

   SEE ALSO
	getbuff, fflush, setvbuf

   (LVO bias -174)

CClib.library/mktemp                                      CClib.library/mktemp
   NAME
	mktemp -- fill a template path with a unique name

   SYNOPSIS
	STRPTR mktemp(STRPTR template);

	Registers: a0=template

   FUNCTION
	Replaces trailing X characters in template with digits derived from process identity and the current DateStamp, then tests whether the path already exists via Lock(). Repeats with different seeds up to 10000 times.

	On success template holds a unused pathname. On exhaustion template[0] is set to '\0'.

   INPUTS
	template - mutable string with trailing XXXXXX pattern

   RESULT
	template pointer (same as input), or NULL if template was NULL.

   NOTES
	Classic insecure temporary name API; tmpnam/tmpfile use T:ccXXXXXX. Not thread-safe across tasks (per-task tempname in tmpnam).

   SEE ALSO
	tmpnam, tmpfile

   (LVO bias -180)

CClib.library/tmpnam                                      CClib.library/tmpnam
   NAME
	tmpnam -- generate a temporary file pathname

   SYNOPSIS
	STRPTR tmpnam(STRPTR s);

	Registers: a0=s

   FUNCTION
	Builds a unique name under T: using the pattern ccXXXXXX and mktemp(). Stores the result in s when s is non-NULL, otherwise in the per-task tempname buffer inside task_UserData (or a static fallback when userdata is absent).

	The returned string is reused on the next tmpnam() call in the same task when s was NULL.

   INPUTS
	s - optional caller buffer (recommended size 32+), or NULL

   RESULT
	STRPTR to the pathname. Caller must not free library-owned storage.

   NOTES
	Creates a name only; open the file yourself. Not safe for concurrent calls with s == NULL.

   SEE ALSO
	mktemp, tmpfile, fopen

   (LVO bias -186)

CClib.library/tmpfile                                    CClib.library/tmpfile
   NAME
	tmpfile -- create a temporary read/write stream

   SYNOPSIS
	APTR tmpfile(void);

   FUNCTION
	Generates a unique T: filename, opens it with fopen() mode w+, and records the path in FILE->_tmpname with CC_TEMP set. fclose() deletes the backing file and frees _tmpname.

	The FILE behaves like any other read/write stream; use fread, fwrite, fseek, and fclose normally.

   INPUTS
	None.

   RESULT
	APTR (FILE *) or NULL if mktemp or fopen failed.

   NOTES
	File is removed on fclose, not on abort unless ClearSTDIO runs closeall.

   SEE ALSO
	tmpnam, mktemp, fclose, freopen

   (LVO bias -192)

CClib.library/putc                                          CClib.library/putc
   NAME
	putc -- write a byte to a stream (macro entry)

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG putc(LONG c, APTR stream);

	Registers: d0=c, a0=stream

   FUNCTION
	Public name for aputc(). Writes the low byte of c to stream, allocating a buffer on first use, flushing when the buffer fills, on newline to interactive streams, or when unbuffered.

	Switching from read to write on the same stream seeks back over unread buffered input before accepting output.

   INPUTS
	c - character (0-255)
	stream - FILE *

   RESULT
	c on success. EOF on error (errno may be EINVAL).

   NOTES
	Prefer aputc at the LVO boundary; putc is the familiar ANSI name.

   SEE ALSO
	aputc, getc, fputs, fwrite

   (LVO bias -198)

CClib.library/aputc                                        CClib.library/aputc
   NAME
	aputc -- buffered put byte on a FILE stream

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG aputc(LONG c, APTR stream);

	Registers: d0=c, a0=stream

   FUNCTION
	Core implementation of character output. Maintains _bp within _buff, sets CC_DIRTY when data is pending, clears CC_EOFBIT, and invokes fflush() when the buffer is full, when c is newline and isatty(_unit) is true, or when _buflen is 1 (unbuffered).

	If the stream was reading, any unread bytes in the buffer are undone with lseek() before writing.

   INPUTS
	c - character value
	stream - FILE *

   RESULT
	c (masked to byte) or EOF.

   NOTES
	puterr and fputs build on aputc.

   SEE ALSO
	putc, fflush, fwrite, putw

   (LVO bias -204)

CClib.library/getc                                          CClib.library/getc
   NAME
	getc -- read a byte from a stream (macro entry)

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG getc(APTR stream);

	Registers: a0=stream

   FUNCTION
	Public alias for agetc(). Returns the next byte from stream, refilling the buffer from read() when exhausted. Sets CC_EOFBIT when the underlying read returns zero or error.

	If the stream has pending output (CC_DIRTY), fflush() runs first.

   INPUTS
	stream - FILE *

   RESULT
	0-255 as LONG, or EOF (-1).

   NOTES
	Macro implementations in client headers may map getc to agetc.

   SEE ALSO
	agetc, ungetc, fread, getchar

   (LVO bias -210)

CClib.library/agetc                                        CClib.library/agetc
   NAME
	agetc -- buffered get byte from a FILE stream

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG agetc(APTR stream);

	Registers: a0=stream

   FUNCTION
	Returns the next input byte, using the stream buffer when data remains between _bp and _bend. Otherwise issues read() for up to _buflen bytes into _buff and returns the first byte of the new chunk.

	Flushes dirty output buffers before reading. On end-of-file or I/O error, sets CC_EOFBIT and optionally CC_IOERR.

   INPUTS
	stream - FILE *

   RESULT
	Character 0-255, or EOF.

   NOTES
	fread and fgets call agetc in a loop.

   SEE ALSO
	getc, ungetc, getbuff

   (LVO bias -216)

CClib.library/getchar                                    CClib.library/getchar
   NAME
	getchar -- read a byte from standard input

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG getchar(void);

   FUNCTION
	Reads one character from *stdin_p in task_UserData. Requires SetupSTDIO to have attached a valid stdin FILE (CLI or WB WINDOW). Equivalent to getc(stdin) when stdin is wired.

	Returns EOF immediately when userdata or stdin is missing.

   INPUTS
	None.

   RESULT
	Byte value or EOF.

   NOTES
	Workbench programs without WINDOW have NULL stdin; do not call blindly.

   SEE ALSO
	agetc, gets, fgets, SetupSTDIO

   (LVO bias -222)

CClib.library/ungetc                                      CClib.library/ungetc
   NAME
	ungetc -- push one character back into a stream

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG ungetc(LONG c, APTR stream);

	Registers: d0=c, a0=stream

   FUNCTION
	Pushes c back so the next agetc() returns it. Only one character of pushback is guaranteed. EOF cannot be pushed.

	If _bp is past _buff, the pointer is decremented. After fseek emptied the buffer (_bp == _bend == _buff), the character is stored as a one-byte lookahead.

   INPUTS
	c - character to push back (not EOF)
	stream - FILE *

   RESULT
	c on success, or EOF if pushback is not possible.

   NOTES
	Cannot push back into a full buffer with no room at the start.

   SEE ALSO
	agetc, fseek

   (LVO bias -228)

CClib.library/putw                                          CClib.library/putw
   NAME
	putw -- write a 16-bit word to a stream

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG putw(ULONG w, APTR stream);

	Registers: d0=w, a0=stream

   FUNCTION
	Writes w as a big-endian 16-bit value: high byte first via aputc(), then low byte. This is the historic Lattice/C word size, not a 32-bit int.

	Stops and returns EOF if either byte write fails.

   INPUTS
	w - 16-bit value (ULONG at LVO)
	stream - FILE *

   RESULT
	w on success, or EOF.

   NOTES
	Binary interchange format is machine-endian for the Amiga 680x0.

   SEE ALSO
	getw, aputc, fwrite

   (LVO bias -234)

CClib.library/puts                                          CClib.library/puts
   NAME
	puts -- write a string and newline to stdout

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG puts(STRPTR s);

	Registers: a0=s

   FUNCTION
	Writes s to *stdout_p using fputs(), then writes a newline with aputc(). Requires SetupSTDIO to have connected stdout.

	Unlike fputs(), puts() always appends '\n' and targets stdout only.

   INPUTS
	s - NUL-terminated string

   RESULT
	Non-negative on success, EOF on failure.

   NOTES
	For stderr or arbitrary streams use fputs() or fprintf LVO.

   SEE ALSO
	fputs, aputc, printf

   (LVO bias -240)

CClib.library/puterr                                      CClib.library/puterr
   NAME
	puterr -- write a byte to standard error

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG puterr(LONG c);

	Registers: d0=c

   FUNCTION
	Writes the low byte of c to *stderr_p via aputc(). Used for diagnostics when stderr is separate from stdout (DOS ErrorOutput when available).

	Returns EOF when stderr was not opened (common for WB tools without WINDOW).

   INPUTS
	c - character

   RESULT
	c or EOF.

   NOTES
	perror() uses fputs to stderr instead of puterr for full lines.

   SEE ALSO
	aputc, perror, SetupSTDIO

   (LVO bias -246)

CClib.library/fwrite                                      CClib.library/fwrite
   NAME
	fwrite -- write formatted blocks to a stream

   SYNOPSIS
	ULONG fwrite(APTR ptr, ULONG size, ULONG nitems, APTR stream);

	Registers: a0=ptr, d0=size, d1=nitems, a1=stream

   FUNCTION
	Writes nitems elements of size bytes each from ptr to stream by calling aputc() for every byte. Stops early on the first output failure.

	This is the block-oriented stdio write; it respects stream buffering and line discipline enforced by aputc().

   INPUTS
	ptr - source data
	size - element size in bytes
	nitems - element count
	stream - FILE *

   RESULT
	Count of complete elements written (may be less than nitems).

   NOTES
	For formatted text, fprintf LVO takes an APTR arg array (RawDoFmt style) instead of C varargs at the library boundary.

   SEE ALSO
	fread, aputc, fprintf

   (LVO bias -252)

CClib.library/perror                                      CClib.library/perror
   NAME
	perror -- print a system error message to stderr

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG perror(STRPTR s);

	Registers: a0=s

   FUNCTION
	Formats a line on stderr: optional prefix s, a colon and space, the strerror() text for the current errno, and a newline. Uses fputs() and aputc() on *stderr_p.

	Typical use after a failed open(), fread(), or other call that set errno through cc_set_errno().

   INPUTS
	s - prefix string (may be empty but not NULL for prefix only)

   RESULT
	0 on success. -1 if stderr is unavailable.

   NOTES
	Reads errno from the pointer stored at SetupSTDIO. Does not clear errno.

   SEE ALSO
	strerror, puterr, fputs

   (LVO bias -258)

CClib.library/fseek                                        CClib.library/fseek
   NAME
	fseek -- reposition a FILE stream

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG fseek(APTR stream, LONG offset, LONG whence);

	Registers: a0=stream, d0=offset, d1=whence

   FUNCTION
	Flushes pending output with fflush(), discards read-ahead by resetting _bp and _bend to _buff, clears EOF and dirty flags, then calls lseek() on the underlying FileDesc.

	Relative and absolute seeks use the same whence constants as lseek(). After fseek, ungetc may plant pushback in an empty buffer.

   INPUTS
	stream - FILE *
	offset - byte offset
	whence - SEEK_SET, SEEK_CUR, or SEEK_END

   RESULT
	0 on success. -1 if fflush, lseek, or validation failed.

   NOTES
	Input buffers are emptied, not preserved across seek.

   SEE ALSO
	ftell, lseek, fflush, ungetc

   (LVO bias -264)

CClib.library/ftell                                        CClib.library/ftell
   NAME
	ftell -- report the current stream position

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG ftell(APTR stream);

	Registers: a0=stream

   FUNCTION
	Returns the logical file position for stream, adjusted for buffered data. Queries lseek(fd, 0, SEEK_CUR) on the underlying FileDesc, then adds pending output bytes (dirty buffer) or subtracts unread input bytes in the buffer.

	Matches the position fseek would use for SEEK_SET offsets.

   INPUTS
	stream - FILE *

   RESULT
	Byte offset, or -1 with errno set.

   NOTES
	Use fgetpos/fsetpos when a wider pos type is required.

   SEE ALSO
	fseek, fgetpos, lseek

   (LVO bias -270)

CClib.library/fflush                                      CClib.library/fflush
   NAME
	fflush -- flush stream buffers to the file

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG fflush(APTR stream);

	Registers: a0=stream

   FUNCTION
	For output streams with CC_DIRTY set, writes the bytes between _buff and _bp through write() on the FileDesc, then resets the buffer pointers. Returns EOF if the write count differs from the buffer length.

	For input streams, discards unread buffered data by seeking the file pointer backward over the lookahead region. Called internally before fseek and when switching from read to write.

   INPUTS
	stream - FILE * (NULL is a no-op success)

   RESULT
	0 on success. EOF on write failure (CC_IOERR set on stream).

   NOTES
	fflush(NULL) is not special here; pass a real stream.

   SEE ALSO
	fseek, aputc, fclose, write

   (LVO bias -276)

CClib.library/getw                                          CClib.library/getw
   NAME
	getw -- read a 16-bit word from a stream

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG getw(APTR stream);

	Registers: a0=stream

   FUNCTION
	Reads two bytes via agetc() and combines them into a 16-bit value (high byte first). Returns EOF if either byte read fails.

	Pair with putw() for symmetric binary word I/O.

   INPUTS
	stream - FILE *

   RESULT
	16-bit value as LONG, or EOF.

   NOTES
	Word size is 16 bits, not sizeof(int).

   SEE ALSO
	putw, agetc

   (LVO bias -282)

CClib.library/fread                                        CClib.library/fread
   NAME
	fread -- read blocks from a stream

   SYNOPSIS
	ULONG fread(APTR ptr, ULONG size, ULONG nitems, APTR stream);

	Registers: a0=ptr, d0=size, d1=nitems, a1=stream

   FUNCTION
	Reads up to nitems elements of size bytes from stream into ptr using agetc() in a loop. Stops at EOF or error and returns the count of complete elements read.

	Honors stdio buffering and errno set by underlying read().

   INPUTS
	ptr - destination buffer
	size - element size
	nitems - element count
	stream - FILE *

   RESULT
	Number of elements read (0 on immediate EOF).

   NOTES
	Text line input is usually fgets(); formatted input uses fscanf LVO with an APTR arg array.

   SEE ALSO
	fwrite, agetc, fgets

   (LVO bias -288)

CClib.library/gets                                          CClib.library/gets
   NAME
	gets -- read a line from stdin without a length limit

   SYNOPSIS
	STRPTR gets(STRPTR s);

	Registers: a0=s

   FUNCTION
	Reads bytes from *stdin_p until newline or EOF, storing into s without a maximum length check. The newline is discarded; a NUL terminator is written.

	Returns NULL on immediate EOF with no data read. This is the classic unsafe gets(); prefer fgets() when the buffer size is known.

   INPUTS
	s - caller buffer large enough for the line

   RESULT
	s on success, NULL on EOF/error or missing stdin.

   NOTES
	Deprecated in modern C standards but provided for Lattice compatibility.

   SEE ALSO
	fgets, getchar, agetc

   (LVO bias -294)

CClib.library/fgets                                        CClib.library/fgets
   NAME
	fgets -- read a line from a stream with a size limit

   SYNOPSIS
	STRPTR fgets(STRPTR s, LONG n, APTR stream);

	Registers: a0=s, d0=n, a1=stream

   FUNCTION
	Reads at most n-1 characters from stream into s, stopping at newline or EOF. Always NUL-terminates s when n > 0. The newline, if read, is kept in the buffer.

	Returns NULL only when EOF occurs before any character is read.

   INPUTS
	s - output buffer
	n - buffer size (including NUL)
	stream - FILE *

   RESULT
	s on success, NULL on EOF with empty line.

   NOTES
	Safer than gets(); typical pattern for CLI line input.

   SEE ALSO
	gets, agetc, fread

   (LVO bias -300)

CClib.library/fputs                                        CClib.library/fputs
   NAME
	fputs -- write a string to a stream

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG fputs(STRPTR s, APTR stream);

	Registers: a0=s, a1=stream

   FUNCTION
	Writes every character of s to stream via aputc() until the terminating NUL (which is not written). Does not append a newline; use puts() for stdout with newline.

	Used by perror() and many internal formatters.

   INPUTS
	s - NUL-terminated string
	stream - FILE *

   RESULT
	Non-negative character count on success, EOF on failure.

   NOTES
	For formatted output use fprintf LVO (APTR arg array, RawDoFmt style) from client stubs.

   SEE ALSO
	puts, aputc, fprintf, perror

   (LVO bias -306)

CClib.library/scanf                                        CClib.library/scanf
   NAME
	scanf -- read formatted input from stdin

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG scanf(STRPTR format, APTR args);

	Registers: a0=format, a1=args

   FUNCTION
	Reads formatted input from the task's stdin stream under control of a format string. At the LVO boundary the call takes a format pointer and an APTR args array in RawDoFmt style; the C compiler wraps variable arguments into that array. Whitespace in the format is skipped in the input. Literal characters must match the next non-whitespace input character. Each conversion reads a field and, unless suppressed with '*', stores into the next pointer taken from the args array. Integer conversions %d, %i, %u, %x, %X, %o and %p store a 32-bit LONG by default on the Amiga; prefix %h stores a short, and %l or %ld stores a long. Floating conversions %e, %f and %g read a DOUBLE; the args array must supply two consecutive LONG words for the IEEE value, and mathieeedoubbas must be available through SetupSTDIO mathbase.

   INPUTS
	STRPTR format - conversion string.
	APTR args - pointer to an array of argument pointers (RawDoFmt layout).

   RESULT
	LONG - number of input items converted and assigned, or EOF if no assignment was made before end of input or a matching failure.

   NOTES
	C callers use scanf(fmt, &a, &b, ...); the library LVO receives only format and args.

   SEE ALSO
	printf, fscanf, fprintf, sscanf, sprintf, SetupSTDIO

   (LVO bias -312)

CClib.library/printf                                      CClib.library/printf
   NAME
	printf -- write formatted output to stdout

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG printf(STRPTR format, APTR args);

	Registers: a0=format, a1=args

   FUNCTION
	Writes formatted output to the task's stdout stream. The LVO takes a format string and an APTR args array (RawDoFmt style), not a C varargs stack frame. Ordinary characters are copied. Each '%' conversion pulls the next value from the args array: pointers to LONG for %d, %u, %x and friends (32-bit default), pointers to two LONG words for %e, %f and %g DOUBLE values, and STRPTR for %s. Floating formats use mathieeedoubbas from the task mathbase set by SetupSTDIO; without mathbase, floating output is limited. Field width, precision, and justification flags follow the usual printf rules.

   INPUTS
	STRPTR format - output format string.
	APTR args - pointer to an array of argument pointers.

   RESULT
	LONG - number of characters written, or -1 on error.

   SEE ALSO
	scanf, fscanf, fprintf, sscanf, sprintf, vprintf

   (LVO bias -318)

CClib.library/fscanf                                      CClib.library/fscanf
   NAME
	fscanf -- read formatted input from a stream

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG fscanf(APTR stream, STRPTR format, APTR args);

	Registers: a0=stream, a1=format, a2=args

   FUNCTION
	Like scanf but reads from the given buffered FILE stream instead of stdin. The LVO receives the stream handle, format string, and an APTR args array in RawDoFmt style. Conversion rules, integer width defaults (%d/%u/%x as 32-bit LONG, %h for short, %ld for long), and DOUBLE handling (%e/%f/%g as two LONG words via mathieeedoubbas) are the same as scanf.

   INPUTS
	APTR stream - FILE pointer opened with fopen or fdopen.
	STRPTR format - conversion string.
	APTR args - pointer to an array of argument pointers.

   RESULT
	LONG - number of items assigned, or EOF on failure before any assignment.

   SEE ALSO
	scanf, printf, fprintf, sscanf, sprintf

   (LVO bias -324)

CClib.library/fprintf                                    CClib.library/fprintf
   NAME
	fprintf -- write formatted output to a stream

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG fprintf(APTR stream, STRPTR format, APTR args);

	Registers: a0=stream, a1=format, a2=args

   FUNCTION
	Like printf but writes to the specified FILE stream. The LVO passes stream, format, and an APTR args array (RawDoFmt style). Each conversion consumes the next entry from args: LONG pointers for integers (32-bit by default, %h short, %ld long), two LONG words per DOUBLE for %e/%f/%g (requires SetupSTDIO mathbase), and strings for %s. Returns the number of characters written to the stream buffer (which may be flushed later).

   INPUTS
	APTR stream - destination FILE pointer.
	STRPTR format - output format string.
	APTR args - pointer to an array of argument pointers.

   RESULT
	LONG - characters written, or -1 on error.

   SEE ALSO
	printf, scanf, fscanf, sscanf, sprintf, vfprintf

   (LVO bias -330)

CClib.library/sscanf                                      CClib.library/sscanf
   NAME
	sscanf -- read formatted input from a string

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG sscanf(STRPTR s, STRPTR format, APTR args);

	Registers: a0=s, a1=format, a2=args

   FUNCTION
	Like scanf but takes input characters from a memory string rather than a stream. The LVO receives the source string, format, and APTR args array. Scanning stops at the string terminator. Integer and floating conversion rules match scanf: 32-bit LONG by default, %h for short, %ld for long, DOUBLE as two LONGs in args for %e/%f/%g with mathieeedoubbas from SetupSTDIO.

   INPUTS
	STRPTR s - NUL-terminated input string.
	STRPTR format - conversion string.
	APTR args - pointer to an array of argument pointers.

   RESULT
	LONG - number of items assigned, or EOF if none.

   SEE ALSO
	scanf, printf, fscanf, fprintf, sprintf

   (LVO bias -336)

CClib.library/sprintf                                    CClib.library/sprintf
   NAME
	sprintf -- write formatted output to a string

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG sprintf(STRPTR s, STRPTR format, APTR args);

	Registers: a0=s, a1=format, a2=args

   FUNCTION
	Formats output into a caller-supplied character buffer and appends a NUL terminator. The LVO takes the destination buffer, format string, and APTR args array (RawDoFmt style). Conversions follow printf rules: LONG pointers for integers (32-bit default, %h short, %ld long), two LONG words per DOUBLE for %e/%f/%g using mathieeedoubbas when mathbase is set. The returned count excludes the terminating NUL.

   INPUTS
	STRPTR s - destination buffer; must be large enough for output plus NUL.
	STRPTR format - output format string.
	APTR args - pointer to an array of argument pointers.

   RESULT
	LONG - number of characters written excluding the NUL terminator, or -1 on error.

   NOTES
	No bounds checking is performed; buffer overrun is the caller's responsibility.

   SEE ALSO
	printf, scanf, fprintf, fscanf, sscanf, vsprintf

   (LVO bias -342)

CClib.library/_alloc                                      CClib.library/_alloc
   NAME
	_alloc -- lowest-level per-task AllocMem

   SYNOPSIS
	APTR _alloc(APTR ud, LONG size, LONG flags);

	Registers: a0=ud, d0=size, d1=flags

   FUNCTION
	Allocates memory from Exec AllocMem and links the block on the calling task's RAM list in task_UserData. This is the lowest allocation layer used by sbrk, malloc, and internal bookkeeping. Each block is prefixed with a MemBlk header; the returned pointer points past that header. Obtain the task_UserData pointer with GetSTDIO. The flags argument is passed directly to AllocMem (for example MEMF_ANY, or MEMF_CHIP for chip RAM accessible to custom chips).

   INPUTS
	APTR ud - task_UserData from GetSTDIO.
	LONG size - payload bytes requested (header added internally).
	LONG flags - Exec AllocMem requirement flags.

   RESULT
	APTR - pointer to allocated payload, or NULL if AllocMem failed.

   SEE ALSO
	_fre, _freall, sbrk, malloc, GetSTDIO

   (LVO bias -348)

CClib.library/_fre                                          CClib.library/_fre
   NAME
	_fre -- free an _alloc or sbrk block

   SYNOPSIS
	void _fre(APTR ud, APTR ptr);

	Registers: a0=ud, a1=ptr

   FUNCTION
	Frees a block previously returned by _alloc or sbrk for the given task_UserData. The pointer must be the payload address (after the internal MemBlk header). The block is unlinked from ud->RAM and returned to Exec with FreeMem.

   INPUTS
	APTR ud - task_UserData from GetSTDIO.
	APTR ptr - payload pointer from _alloc or sbrk.

   RESULT
	None.

   NOTES
	Do not pass pointers from malloc/free; those use a different header layout.

   SEE ALSO
	_alloc, _freall, sbrk, free

   (LVO bias -354)

CClib.library/_freall                                    CClib.library/_freall
   NAME
	_freall -- free every block on the RAM list

   SYNOPSIS
	void _freall(APTR ud);

	Registers: a0=ud

   FUNCTION
	Walks ud->RAM and FreeMems every MemBlk allocated through _alloc or sbrk for this task. Does not reset the K&R heap free list used by malloc; use freeall for a full teardown including heap state and open file lists.

   INPUTS
	APTR ud - task_UserData from GetSTDIO.

   RESULT
	None.

   SEE ALSO
	_alloc, _fre, freeall, sbrk

   (LVO bias -360)

CClib.library/sbrk                                          CClib.library/sbrk
   NAME
	sbrk -- grow the task RAM arena

   SYNOPSIS
	APTR sbrk(LONG incr);

	Registers: d0=incr

   FUNCTION
	Allocates incr bytes through _alloc for the current task (GetSTDIO task_UserData) with MEMF_ANY and returns the payload pointer. Blocks are recorded on the task RAM list like other _alloc requests. This is the historic Unix-style arena grow used beneath the heap manager.

   INPUTS
	LONG incr - number of bytes to add to the RAM list.

   RESULT
	APTR - pointer to new memory, or (APTR)-1 if allocation failed.

   NOTES
	Free with _fre using the same task_UserData, not free().

   SEE ALSO
	_alloc, _fre, malloc, heap_alloc

   (LVO bias -366)

CClib.library/heap_alloc                              CClib.library/heap_alloc
   NAME
	heap_alloc -- allocate from the K&R heap free list

   SYNOPSIS
	APTR heap_alloc(ULONG size);

	Registers: d0=size

   FUNCTION
	Allocates from the per-task heap manager. If a be_free donation on lastfree is large enough, that static block is reused once; otherwise the request is satisfied by malloc. Used internally to implement malloc and calloc. Heap memory is drawn from FAST RAM first, then CHIP, via the underlying _alloc path.

   INPUTS
	ULONG size - minimum payload bytes required.

   RESULT
	APTR - pointer to allocated block, or NULL on failure.

   SEE ALSO
	heap_free, malloc, calloc, be_free

   (LVO bias -372)

CClib.library/heap_free                                CClib.library/heap_free
   NAME
	heap_free -- return a block to the heap manager

   SYNOPSIS
	void heap_free(APTR ptr);

	Registers: a0=ptr

   FUNCTION
	Returns memory obtained from heap_alloc (or malloc/calloc/realloc) to the per-task heap. In this implementation heap_free is an alias for free, which releases the underlying _alloc block via the malloc header.

   INPUTS
	APTR ptr - pointer from heap_alloc, malloc, calloc, or realloc.

   RESULT
	None.

   SEE ALSO
	heap_alloc, free, malloc

   (LVO bias -378)

CClib.library/malloc                                      CClib.library/malloc
   NAME
	malloc -- allocate bytes from the task heap

   SYNOPSIS
	APTR malloc(ULONG size);

	Registers: d0=size

   FUNCTION
	Allocates at least size bytes from the calling task's heap (task_UserData). The requested size is rounded up to a multiple of eight bytes; malloc_size reports the rounded value. Memory is obtained from Exec via _alloc, trying FAST RAM before CHIP. A ULONG size word precedes the returned pointer. Sets errno to ENOMEM on failure.

   INPUTS
	ULONG size - minimum number of payload bytes.

   RESULT
	APTR - pointer to allocated memory, or NULL if unavailable.

   NOTES
	malloc(0) may return NULL or a unique freeable pointer depending on availability; both are valid historic behaviour.

   SEE ALSO
	calloc, realloc, free, malloc_size, heap_alloc

   (LVO bias -384)

CClib.library/calloc                                      CClib.library/calloc
   NAME
	calloc -- allocate and zero an array

   SYNOPSIS
	APTR calloc(ULONG nelem, ULONG elsize);

	Registers: d0=nelem, d1=elsize

   FUNCTION
	Allocates nelem * elsize bytes from the per-task heap (same FAST then CHIP policy as malloc), then clears every byte to zero. Uses malloc internally; returns NULL if the product overflows or allocation fails.

   INPUTS
	ULONG nelem - number of elements.
	ULONG elsize - size of each element in bytes.

   RESULT
	APTR - pointer to zeroed block, or NULL on failure.

   SEE ALSO
	malloc, realloc, free

   (LVO bias -390)

CClib.library/realloc                                    CClib.library/realloc
   NAME
	realloc -- resize a heap block

   SYNOPSIS
	APTR realloc(APTR ptr, ULONG size);

	Registers: a0=ptr, d0=size

   FUNCTION
	Changes the size of a block previously obtained from malloc, calloc, or realloc. Contents are preserved up to the smaller of the old and new sizes. A new block is allocated and the old one freed; the address may change. Passing NULL is equivalent to malloc(size); passing size 0 frees ptr and returns NULL.

   INPUTS
	APTR ptr - existing block, or NULL for a fresh allocation.
	ULONG size - new minimum payload size (8-byte rounded).

   RESULT
	APTR - pointer to resized block, or NULL on failure (original block left intact when allocation fails).

   SEE ALSO
	malloc, calloc, free, malloc_size

   (LVO bias -396)

CClib.library/free                                          CClib.library/free
   NAME
	free -- release a heap allocation

   SYNOPSIS
	void free(APTR ptr);

	Registers: a0=ptr

   FUNCTION
	Returns a block allocated by malloc, calloc, or realloc to the per-task heap manager. The pointer is adjusted back to the internal header and released through _fre on the current task's task_UserData. NULL is ignored safely.

   INPUTS
	APTR ptr - payload pointer from malloc family.

   RESULT
	None.

   NOTES
	Do not free pointers returned by remv_head and friends until you have finished using the detached node; free the node pointer, not only the data pointer.

   SEE ALSO
	malloc, calloc, realloc, heap_free

   (LVO bias -402)

CClib.library/be_free                                    CClib.library/be_free
   NAME
	be_free -- donate static RAM to the heap

   SYNOPSIS
	void be_free(APTR ptr, ULONG size);

	Registers: a0=ptr, d0=size

   FUNCTION
	Registers a block of static memory with the heap manager so a subsequent heap_alloc or malloc of suitable size can reuse it once. The memory must not be written after donation. ptr was not obtained from AllocMem; it is never FreeMem'd by the library. Only the most recent donation on lastfree is remembered.

   INPUTS
	APTR ptr - start of static buffer to donate.
	ULONG size - size of the buffer in bytes (rounded up internally).

   RESULT
	None.

   NOTES
	Writing into a donated block can corrupt heap bookkeeping.

   SEE ALSO
	heap_alloc, malloc, free

   (LVO bias -408)

CClib.library/malloc_size                            CClib.library/malloc_size
   NAME
	malloc_size -- query an allocated block size

   SYNOPSIS
	ULONG malloc_size(APTR ptr);

	Registers: a0=ptr

   FUNCTION
	Returns the size in bytes of the payload region for a pointer obtained from malloc, calloc, realloc, or heap_alloc. The stored size is the value rounded up to eight bytes at allocation time, which may be larger than the size originally requested.

   INPUTS
	APTR ptr - payload pointer from the malloc family.

   RESULT
	ULONG - rounded payload size, or 0 if ptr is NULL.

   SEE ALSO
	malloc, calloc, realloc, heap_alloc

   (LVO bias -414)

CClib.library/freeall                                    CClib.library/freeall
   NAME
	freeall -- release all task heap and buffers

   SYNOPSIS
	void freeall(void);

   FUNCTION
	Calls _freall to return every _alloc arena block to Exec, resets the K&R heap base pointers, and clears the OpenFiles and StreamFiles lists for the current task. Intended for program shutdown (for example from exit); call closeall first so open BPTRs are released while FILE structures still exist.

   INPUTS
	None.

   RESULT
	None.

   NOTES
	After freeall, previously returned pointers must not be used.

   SEE ALSO
	_freall, free, closeall, exit

   (LVO bias -420)

CClib.library/inst_node                                CClib.library/inst_node
   NAME
	inst_node -- allocate a cleared list node

   SYNOPSIS
	APTR inst_node(LONG size);

	Registers: d0=size

   FUNCTION
	Allocates and zero-fills a list node plus size bytes of caller data using Exec AllocMem (not the malloc heap). Layout is [ULONG total][cc_node][data]. Returns a pointer to the cc_node structure; caller data begins at (node + 1). Prev/next are NULL. Link with put_head or put_tail, or use add_head/add_tail to allocate and link in one step.

   INPUTS
	LONG size - bytes of payload data after the node header.

   RESULT
	APTR - pointer to cc_node, or NULL if AllocMem failed.

   NOTES
	Free with FreeMem: hp = (ULONG *)node - 1; FreeMem(hp, *hp).

   SEE ALSO
	add_head, add_tail, put_head, put_tail, remv_node

   (LVO bias -426)

CClib.library/put_head                                  CClib.library/put_head
   NAME
	put_head -- link a node at list head

   SYNOPSIS
	void put_head(APTR list, APTR node);

	Registers: a0=list, a1=node

   FUNCTION
	Inserts an existing node at the beginning of a cc_list. Updates first, last, and length. The node must have been allocated with inst_node or add_head/add_tail and must not already be on a list.

   INPUTS
	APTR list - pointer to cc_list header.
	APTR node - pointer to cc_node from inst_node.

   RESULT
	None.

   SEE ALSO
	put_tail, add_head, remv_head, inst_node

   (LVO bias -432)

CClib.library/add_head                                  CClib.library/add_head
   NAME
	add_head -- allocate a node at list head

   SYNOPSIS
	APTR add_head(APTR list, LONG size);

	Registers: a0=list, d0=size

   FUNCTION
	Combines inst_node and put_head: AllocMems a cleared node with size bytes of payload, links it at the head of the list, and returns a pointer to the data area (node + 1), not the node pointer itself.

   INPUTS
	APTR list - pointer to cc_list header.
	LONG size - payload data bytes for the new node.

   RESULT
	APTR - pointer to node data, or NULL on allocation failure.

   SEE ALSO
	add_tail, inst_node, put_head, remv_head

   (LVO bias -438)

CClib.library/put_tail                                  CClib.library/put_tail
   NAME
	put_tail -- link a node at list tail

   SYNOPSIS
	void put_tail(APTR list, APTR node);

	Registers: a0=list, a1=node

   FUNCTION
	Appends an existing node to the end of a cc_list. Updates first when the list was empty, always updates last and length.

   INPUTS
	APTR list - pointer to cc_list header.
	APTR node - pointer to cc_node from inst_node.

   RESULT
	None.

   SEE ALSO
	put_head, add_tail, remv_tail, inst_node

   (LVO bias -444)

CClib.library/add_tail                                  CClib.library/add_tail
   NAME
	add_tail -- allocate a node at list tail

   SYNOPSIS
	APTR add_tail(APTR list, LONG size);

	Registers: a0=list, d0=size

   FUNCTION
	AllocMems a cleared node with size bytes of payload, links it at the tail of the list, and returns a pointer to the data area (node + 1).

   INPUTS
	APTR list - pointer to cc_list header.
	LONG size - payload data bytes for the new node.

   RESULT
	APTR - pointer to node data, or NULL on allocation failure.

   SEE ALSO
	add_head, put_tail, remv_tail, inst_node

   (LVO bias -450)

CClib.library/remv_head                                CClib.library/remv_head
   NAME
	remv_head -- detach the first list node

   SYNOPSIS
	APTR remv_head(APTR list, APTR datap);

	Registers: a0=list, a1=datap

   FUNCTION
	Removes the first node from the list without freeing memory. If datap is non-NULL, stores a pointer to the node's data (node + 1) at *datap. Returns the cc_node pointer so the caller can FreeMem using the size prefix: hp = (ULONG *)node - 1; FreeMem(hp, *hp).

   INPUTS
	APTR list - pointer to cc_list header.
	APTR datap - optional pointer to APTR to receive data address.

   RESULT
	APTR - detached cc_node, or NULL if the list is empty.

   SEE ALSO
	remv_tail, remv_node, r_node, add_head

   (LVO bias -456)

CClib.library/remv_tail                                CClib.library/remv_tail
   NAME
	remv_tail -- detach the last list node

   SYNOPSIS
	APTR remv_tail(APTR list, APTR datap);

	Registers: a0=list, a1=datap

   FUNCTION
	Removes the last node from the list without freeing memory. Optionally stores the data pointer at *datap. Returns the node pointer for FreeMem via the ULONG size word immediately before the cc_node.

   INPUTS
	APTR list - pointer to cc_list header.
	APTR datap - optional pointer to APTR to receive data address.

   RESULT
	APTR - detached cc_node, or NULL if the list is empty.

   SEE ALSO
	remv_head, remv_node, r_node, add_tail

   (LVO bias -462)

CClib.library/remv_list                                CClib.library/remv_list
   NAME
	remv_list -- free an entire list and its header

   SYNOPSIS
	void remv_list(APTR list);

	Registers: a0=list

   FUNCTION
	Walks the list, FreeMeming every node using the size prefix before each cc_node, then FreeMems the list header itself as sizeof(cc_list). The header must have been AllocMem'd to that size. After the call the list pointer is invalid.

   INPUTS
	APTR list - pointer to cc_list header (AllocMem allocated).

   RESULT
	None.

   NOTES
	All nodes must use the inst_node/add_head layout with a leading ULONG total size.

   SEE ALSO
	remv_head, remv_tail, remv_node, inst_node

   (LVO bias -468)

CClib.library/r_node                                      CClib.library/r_node
   NAME
	r_node -- detach a known node from a list

   SYNOPSIS
	APTR r_node(APTR list, APTR node, APTR datap);

	Registers: a0=list, a1=node, a2=datap

   FUNCTION
	Unlinks a specific cc_node from the list. If datap is non-NULL, stores the data pointer (node + 1) at *datap. Returns the node pointer without freeing; release with FreeMem and the size prefix.

   INPUTS
	APTR list - pointer to cc_list header.
	APTR node - cc_node to remove.
	APTR datap - optional pointer to APTR to receive data address.

   RESULT
	APTR - the detached node, or NULL if list or node is invalid.

   SEE ALSO
	remv_node, remv_head, remv_tail

   (LVO bias -474)

CClib.library/remv_node                                CClib.library/remv_node
   NAME
	remv_node -- detach a node by its data pointer

   SYNOPSIS
	APTR remv_node(APTR list, APTR data);

	Registers: a0=list, a1=data

   FUNCTION
	Scans the list for a node whose data area (node + 1) matches data, unlinks it, and returns the cc_node pointer. Does not free memory; FreeMem using hp = (ULONG *)node - 1 and size *hp.

   INPUTS
	APTR list - pointer to cc_list header.
	APTR data - pointer returned by add_head or add_tail.

   RESULT
	APTR - detached cc_node, or NULL if no matching data was found.

   SEE ALSO
	r_node, remv_head, remv_tail

   (LVO bias -480)

CClib.library/atoi                                          CClib.library/atoi
   NAME
	atoi -- convert string to integer

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG atoi(STRPTR s);

	Registers: a0=s

   FUNCTION
	Parses a decimal integer from a NUL-terminated string. Leading white space is skipped. An optional sign is accepted. Parsing stops at the first non-digit. Implemented as a call to atol; on Amiga LONG and int are both 32-bit.

   INPUTS
	STRPTR s - string to convert.

   RESULT
	LONG - converted value, or 0 if no conversion could be performed.

   SEE ALSO
	atol, atof, ltoa, stoa

   (LVO bias -486)

CClib.library/atol                                          CClib.library/atol
   NAME
	atol -- convert string to long integer

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG atol(STRPTR s);

	Registers: a0=s

   FUNCTION
	Parses a signed decimal long from s, skipping leading white space and an optional '+' or '-' prefix. Accumulates digits until a non-digit is seen. Returns zero when no digits were read.

   INPUTS
	STRPTR s - string to convert.

   RESULT
	LONG - converted value, or 0 if no conversion could be performed.

   SEE ALSO
	atoi, atof, ltoa

   (LVO bias -492)

CClib.library/atof                                          CClib.library/atof
   NAME
	atof -- convert string to IEEE double

   SYNOPSIS
	DOUBLE atof(STRPTR s);

	Registers: a0=s

   FUNCTION
	Parses a floating-point literal from a string into an IEEE double-precision value. When SetupSTDIO has installed mathieeedoubbas as mathbase, conversion uses the IEEE library for full accuracy. Without mathbase, a fixed-point fallback builds IEEE bits using integer arithmetic only.

   INPUTS
	STRPTR s - string to convert.

   RESULT
	DOUBLE - converted value, or 0.0 if no conversion could be made.

   NOTES
	Requires SetupSTDIO mathbase for mathieeedoubbas IEEE helpers.

   SEE ALSO
	atoi, atol, ltoa, SetupSTDIO

   (LVO bias -498)

CClib.library/ltoa                                          CClib.library/ltoa
   NAME
	ltoa -- convert long integer to decimal string

   SYNOPSIS
	void ltoa(LONG n, STRPTR s);

	Registers: d0=n, a0=s

   FUNCTION
	Writes a signed decimal representation of n into the caller-supplied buffer s as a NUL-terminated string. Handles 0x80000000 explicitly. The buffer must be large enough for the sign, up to ten digits, and the terminator (typically 12 bytes).

   INPUTS
	LONG n - value to convert.
	STRPTR s - destination buffer written by the function.

   RESULT
	None.

   SEE ALSO
	atol, atoi, stoa

   (LVO bias -504)

CClib.library/stoa                                          CClib.library/stoa
   NAME
	stoa -- convert short integer to decimal string

   SYNOPSIS
	void stoa(LONG n, STRPTR s);

	Registers: d0=n, a0=s

   FUNCTION
	Converts a short integer n to a decimal string in the caller-supplied buffer s by casting to LONG and calling ltoa. Buffer sizing matches ltoa.

   INPUTS
	LONG n - short value passed as LONG in the LVO.
	STRPTR s - destination buffer.

   RESULT
	None.

   SEE ALSO
	atoi, ltoa, atol

   (LVO bias -510)

CClib.library/bcmp                                          CClib.library/bcmp
   NAME
	bcmp -- compare two memory blocks (BSD)

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG bcmp(APTR b1, APTR b2, LONG len);

	Registers: a0=b1, a1=b2, d0=len

   FUNCTION
	Compares the first len bytes of two memory regions byte by byte. Behaves like memcmp in this implementation: returns 0 when all bytes match, a negative LONG if the first differing byte in b1 is less than that in b2, positive if greater. Zero length compares equal without accessing memory.

   INPUTS
	APTR b1 - first region.
	APTR b2 - second region.
	LONG len - number of bytes to compare.

   RESULT
	LONG - less than, equal to, or greater than zero as memcmp.

   SEE ALSO
	memcmp, bcopy, memcpy

   (LVO bias -516)

CClib.library/bcopy                                        CClib.library/bcopy
   NAME
	bcopy -- copy memory with overlap safety

   SYNOPSIS
	void bcopy(APTR src, APTR dst, LONG len);

	Registers: a0=src, a1=dst, d0=len

   FUNCTION
	Copies len bytes from src to dst. When the regions overlap, copy direction is chosen so source data is not clobbered before it is read; non-overlapping copies use Exec CopyMem. Argument order is source then destination (BSD order), opposite to memcpy.

   INPUTS
	APTR src - source address.
	APTR dst - destination address.
	LONG len - number of bytes to copy.

   RESULT
	None.

   SEE ALSO
	memcpy, memmove, bzero

   (LVO bias -522)

CClib.library/bzero                                        CClib.library/bzero
   NAME
	bzero -- zero a memory region

   SYNOPSIS
	void bzero(APTR b, LONG len);

	Registers: a0=b, d0=len

   FUNCTION
	Sets each of the len bytes starting at b to zero. Equivalent to memset(b, 0, len) but provided for BSD compatibility.

   INPUTS
	APTR b - region to clear.
	LONG len - number of bytes to set to zero.

   RESULT
	None.

   SEE ALSO
	memset, bcopy

   (LVO bias -528)

CClib.library/memccpy                                    CClib.library/memccpy
   NAME
	memccpy -- copy bytes until a stop character

   SYNOPSIS
	APTR memccpy(APTR dst, APTR src, ULONG c, LONG n);

	Registers: a0=dst, a1=src, d0=c, d1=n

   FUNCTION
	Copies bytes from src to dst up to n bytes or until a byte equal to c (low eight bits) is copied from src, whichever comes first. Returns a pointer to the byte in dst after the copied stop character, or NULL if c was not found within n bytes.

   INPUTS
	APTR dst - destination.
	APTR src - source.
	ULONG c - stop character (truncated to UBYTE).
	LONG n - maximum bytes to examine.

   RESULT
	APTR - address after the copied stop byte, or NULL if not found.

   SEE ALSO
	memcpy, memchr, bcopy

   (LVO bias -534)

CClib.library/memchr                                      CClib.library/memchr
   NAME
	memchr -- search a buffer for a byte

   SYNOPSIS
	APTR memchr(APTR b, ULONG c, LONG n);

	Registers: a0=b, d0=c, d1=n

   FUNCTION
	Scans the first n bytes of b for the first occurrence of c (low eight bits). Returns a pointer to the matching byte or NULL if c does not appear in the range.

   INPUTS
	APTR b - buffer to search.
	ULONG c - byte value to find.
	LONG n - number of bytes to scan.

   RESULT
	APTR - pointer to first match, or NULL if not found.

   SEE ALSO
	memccpy, strchr, index

   (LVO bias -540)

CClib.library/memcmp                                      CClib.library/memcmp
   NAME
	memcmp -- compare two memory regions

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG memcmp(APTR b1, APTR b2, LONG n);

	Registers: a0=b1, a1=b2, d0=n

   FUNCTION
	Lexicographically compares two regions of n bytes. Returns zero if all bytes are equal, a negative value if the first differing byte in b1 is less than that in b2, or a positive value if greater. Zero length is treated as equal.

   INPUTS
	APTR b1 - first region.
	APTR b2 - second region.
	LONG n - number of bytes to compare.

   RESULT
	LONG - comparison result as for strcmp.

   SEE ALSO
	bcmp, memcpy, bcopy

   (LVO bias -546)

CClib.library/memcpy                                      CClib.library/memcpy
   NAME
	memcpy -- copy a memory block

   SYNOPSIS
	APTR memcpy(APTR dst, APTR src, LONG n);

	Registers: a0=dst, a1=src, d0=n

   FUNCTION
	Copies n bytes from src to dst and returns dst. In this port non-overlapping regions are copied with Exec CopyMem. Callers must ensure regions do not overlap unless bcopy is used; historic CClib documentation documents overlap-safe behaviour for memcpy via the same implementation strategy as bcopy when required.

   INPUTS
	APTR dst - destination.
	APTR src - source.
	LONG n - number of bytes to copy.

   RESULT
	APTR - dst.

   NOTES
	Prefer bcopy when overlap is possible.

   SEE ALSO
	bcopy, memmove, memset

   (LVO bias -552)

CClib.library/memset                                      CClib.library/memset
   NAME
	memset -- fill a memory region with a byte

   SYNOPSIS
	APTR memset(APTR b, ULONG c, LONG n);

	Registers: a0=b, d0=c, d1=n

   FUNCTION
	Writes the low eight bits of c into each of the n bytes starting at b. Returns b. Commonly used to initialise structures and buffers.

   INPUTS
	APTR b - region to fill.
	ULONG c - fill byte (truncated to UBYTE).
	LONG n - number of bytes to set.

   RESULT
	APTR - b.

   SEE ALSO
	bzero, memcpy

   (LVO bias -558)

CClib.library/memswap                                    CClib.library/memswap
   NAME
	memswap -- exchange two memory regions

   SYNOPSIS
	void memswap(APTR b1, APTR b2, LONG n);

	Registers: a0=b1, a1=b2, d0=n

   FUNCTION
	Swaps the contents of two buffers of n bytes byte by byte using a temporary variable. Both pointers must address at least n valid bytes.

   INPUTS
	APTR b1 - first region.
	APTR b2 - second region.
	LONG n - number of bytes to exchange.

   RESULT
	None.

   SEE ALSO
	memcpy, bcopy

   (LVO bias -564)

CClib.library/index                                        CClib.library/index
   NAME
	index -- find first byte in string (BSD alias)

   SYNOPSIS
	STRPTR index(STRPTR s, LONG c);

	Registers: a0=s, d0=c

   FUNCTION
	Scans the NUL-terminated string s from the beginning and returns a pointer to the first occurrence of the byte c (cast to unsigned char). The search includes the terminating NUL when c is zero.

	This is the historic BSD name for strchr. CClib implements index directly rather than calling strchr, so both names are available for legacy sources.

   INPUTS
	s - NUL-terminated string to search
	c - byte value to find (LONG, low 8 bits used)

   RESULT
	Pointer to the matching byte within s, or NULL if c does not occur (or if s is NULL).

   SEE ALSO
	strchr, rindex, strrchr

   (LVO bias -570)

CClib.library/rindex                                      CClib.library/rindex
   NAME
	rindex -- find last byte in string (BSD alias)

   SYNOPSIS
	STRPTR rindex(STRPTR s, LONG c);

	Registers: a0=s, d0=c

   FUNCTION
	Scans the NUL-terminated string s and returns a pointer to the last occurrence of the byte c. If c does not appear, returns NULL. When c is zero, the returned pointer is the terminating NUL of s.

	This is the historic BSD name for strrchr.

   INPUTS
	s - NUL-terminated string to search
	c - byte value to find (LONG, low 8 bits used)

   RESULT
	Pointer to the last matching byte within s, or NULL if not found (or if s is NULL).

   SEE ALSO
	strrchr, index, strchr

   (LVO bias -576)

CClib.library/strupr                                      CClib.library/strupr
   NAME
	strupr -- convert string to uppercase in place

   SYNOPSIS
	STRPTR strupr(STRPTR s);

	Registers: a0=s

   FUNCTION
	Walks the NUL-terminated string s and converts every lowercase letter 'a' through 'z' to the corresponding uppercase letter. All other bytes are left unchanged. The conversion is done in place; the same pointer s is returned.

	This is an Amiga/CClib helper not found in strict ANSI C.

   INPUTS
	s - NUL-terminated string to upper-case

   RESULT
	s (unchanged pointer; contents modified), or s if s is NULL.

   SEE ALSO
	toupper, tolower, strcmpa

   (LVO bias -582)

CClib.library/stpcrlf                                    CClib.library/stpcrlf
   NAME
	stpcrlf -- truncate string at first CR or LF

   SYNOPSIS
	STRPTR stpcrlf(STRPTR s);

	Registers: a0=s

   FUNCTION
	Scans the NUL-terminated string s from the start. When the first carriage return ('\r') or line feed ('\n') is found, that byte is replaced with NUL and scanning stops. Bytes before the line break remain; the remainder of the original string is no longer reachable through s.

	Useful for stripping AmigaDOS or serial line endings from a buffer read by gets or fgets.

   INPUTS
	s - NUL-terminated string to trim

   RESULT
	s (modified in place).

   SEE ALSO
	strcat, strcpy, strlen

   (LVO bias -588)

CClib.library/strcat                                      CClib.library/strcat
   NAME
	strcat -- append one string to another

   SYNOPSIS
	STRPTR strcat(STRPTR dst, STRPTR src);

	Registers: a0=dst, a1=src

   FUNCTION
	Appends the NUL-terminated string src to the end of dst. Finds the existing NUL in dst, then copies src with utility.library/Strncpy (V47) using ULONG_MAX as the size. dst must already contain a valid NUL-terminated string with enough allocated space for the combined result plus a terminating NUL.

   INPUTS
	dst - destination buffer containing an existing string
	src - NUL-terminated string to append

   RESULT
	dst (pointer to the combined string).

   NOTES
	Requires utility.library V47+.

   SEE ALSO
	strcpy, strncat, strlen

   (LVO bias -594)

CClib.library/strchr                                      CClib.library/strchr
   NAME
	strchr -- find first byte in string

   SYNOPSIS
	STRPTR strchr(STRPTR s, LONG c);

	Registers: a0=s, d0=c

   FUNCTION
	Scans the NUL-terminated string s from the beginning and returns a pointer to the first occurrence of the byte c. Identical in behaviour to index; both names are provided for portability.

   INPUTS
	s - NUL-terminated string to search
	c - byte value to find (LONG, low 8 bits used)

   RESULT
	Pointer to the matching byte within s, or NULL if c does not occur (or if s is NULL).

   SEE ALSO
	index, strrchr, rindex

   (LVO bias -600)

CClib.library/strcmp                                      CClib.library/strcmp
   NAME
	strcmp -- compare two strings

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG strcmp(STRPTR s1, STRPTR s2);

	Registers: a0=s1, a1=s2

   FUNCTION
	Compares the NUL-terminated strings s1 and s2 byte by byte using unsigned char values. Scanning stops at the first differing byte or at the first NUL in either string.

	When utility.library is available, Strcmp may be used internally for the same semantics.

   INPUTS
	s1 - first string
	s2 - second string

   RESULT
	Zero if the strings are equal. A positive LONG if s1 is greater than s2, negative if s1 is less. NULL pointers sort before non-NULL pointers.

   SEE ALSO
	strncmp, strcmpa

   (LVO bias -606)

CClib.library/strcpy                                      CClib.library/strcpy
   NAME
	strcpy -- copy a string

   SYNOPSIS
	STRPTR strcpy(STRPTR dst, STRPTR src);

	Registers: a0=dst, a1=src

   FUNCTION
	Copies bytes from the NUL-terminated string src into dst, including the terminating NUL. Implemented with utility.library/Strncpy (V47) using ULONG_MAX as the buffer size, matching unbounded C strcpy. dst must be large enough to hold the full source string plus its terminator.

   INPUTS
	dst - destination buffer
	src - NUL-terminated source string

   RESULT
	dst (pointer to the copied string).

   NOTES
	Requires utility.library V47+. Strncpy's return (end pointer) is discarded; the LVO always returns dst.

   SEE ALSO
	strncpy, strcat, bcpl_strcpy

   (LVO bias -612)

CClib.library/strcspn                                    CClib.library/strcspn
   NAME
	strcspn -- length of initial segment not in reject set

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG strcspn(STRPTR s, STRPTR reject);

	Registers: a0=s, a1=reject

   FUNCTION
	Measures the length of the initial segment of s that contains only bytes not present in the NUL-terminated string reject. Scanning stops at the first byte of s that appears anywhere in reject, or at the NUL terminator of s.

   INPUTS
	s - string to scan
	reject - NUL-terminated set of bytes to reject

   RESULT
	Number of bytes in the initial segment of s before any reject character (0 if s is NULL).

   SEE ALSO
	strspn, strpbrk, strbrk

   (LVO bias -618)

CClib.library/strlen                                      CClib.library/strlen
   NAME
	strlen -- length of a string

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG strlen(STRPTR s);

	Registers: a0=s

   FUNCTION
	Counts bytes in the NUL-terminated string s up to but not including the terminating NUL.

   INPUTS
	s - NUL-terminated string

   RESULT
	Number of bytes before the terminator (0 if s is NULL).

   SEE ALSO
	strcpy, strcat, strncmp

   (LVO bias -624)

CClib.library/strncat                                    CClib.library/strncat
   NAME
	strncat -- append up to n bytes from a string

   SYNOPSIS
	STRPTR strncat(STRPTR dst, STRPTR src, LONG n);

	Registers: a0=dst, a1=src, d0=n

   FUNCTION
	Appends at most n bytes from src to the end of dst, then writes a terminating NUL. If src is shorter than n bytes, copying stops at the NUL in src. dst must already contain a NUL-terminated string with room for the appended bytes and a new terminator.

   INPUTS
	dst - destination buffer with existing string
	src - source string
	n - maximum number of bytes to copy from src

   RESULT
	dst.

   NOTES
	Not utility.library/Strncat: that call takes the full destination buffer size, not C's append count.

   SEE ALSO
	strcat, strncpy, strcpy

   (LVO bias -630)

CClib.library/strcmpa                                    CClib.library/strcmpa
   NAME
	strcmpa -- case-insensitive string compare (Amiga)

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG strcmpa(STRPTR s1, STRPTR s2);

	Registers: a0=s1, a1=s2

   FUNCTION
	Compares s1 and s2 without regard to letter case. When utility.library is open, Stricmp is used. Otherwise CClib folds ASCII 'A'-'Z' to lowercase before comparing unsigned bytes.

	This is the Amiga equivalent of strcasecmp / stricmp.

   INPUTS
	s1 - first string
	s2 - second string

   RESULT
	Zero if equal ignoring case; positive or negative LONG if they differ (same ordering rules as strcmp).

   SEE ALSO
	strcmp, strncmp, strupr

   (LVO bias -636)

CClib.library/strncmp                                    CClib.library/strncmp
   NAME
	strncmp -- compare at most n bytes of two strings

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG strncmp(STRPTR s1, STRPTR s2, LONG n);

	Registers: a0=s1, a1=s2, d0=n

   FUNCTION
	Compares at most n bytes of s1 and s2 as unsigned characters. If all n bytes match and neither string ended early, returns zero. Stops at the first mismatch or at a NUL in either string within the first n bytes.

   INPUTS
	s1 - first string
	s2 - second string
	n - maximum number of bytes to compare

   RESULT
	Zero if the compared portions are equal; otherwise a positive or negative difference of the first unequal bytes. Returns 0 when n <= 0.

   SEE ALSO
	strcmp, strcmpa, strncpy

   (LVO bias -642)

CClib.library/strncpy                                    CClib.library/strncpy
   NAME
	strncpy -- copy at most n bytes (ANSI padding)

   SYNOPSIS
	STRPTR strncpy(STRPTR dst, STRPTR src, LONG n);

	Registers: a0=dst, a1=src, d0=n

   FUNCTION
	Copies at most n bytes from src into dst. If src is shorter than n, the remainder of the n-byte region is filled with NUL bytes (ANSI strncpy). If src is longer than or equal to n, exactly n bytes are stored and no extra terminator is written beyond that region.

	When n is 0, CClib still writes a single NUL at dst[0] and copies no source bytes (historic CClib behaviour).

   INPUTS
	dst - destination buffer
	src - source string
	n - size of the destination region to fill

   RESULT
	dst.

   NOTES
	Not utility.library/Strncpy: that API takes destination buffer size and always NUL-terminates inside the buffer.

   SEE ALSO
	strcpy, strncat

   (LVO bias -648)

CClib.library/strpbrk                                    CClib.library/strpbrk
   NAME
	strpbrk -- find first byte from a set in a string

   SYNOPSIS
	STRPTR strpbrk(STRPTR s, STRPTR accept);

	Registers: a0=s, a1=accept

   FUNCTION
	Scans s from the beginning and returns a pointer to the first byte that also appears in the NUL-terminated string accept.

   INPUTS
	s - string to scan
	accept - NUL-terminated set of bytes to match

   RESULT
	Pointer to the first matching byte in s, or NULL if no accept byte occurs (or if s or accept is NULL).

   SEE ALSO
	strcspn, strspn, strchr

   (LVO bias -654)

CClib.library/strrchr                                    CClib.library/strrchr
   NAME
	strrchr -- find last byte in string

   SYNOPSIS
	STRPTR strrchr(STRPTR s, LONG c);

	Registers: a0=s, d0=c

   FUNCTION
	Scans the NUL-terminated string s and returns a pointer to the last occurrence of byte c. Identical in behaviour to rindex.

   INPUTS
	s - NUL-terminated string to search
	c - byte value to find (LONG, low 8 bits used)

   RESULT
	Pointer to the last matching byte within s, or NULL if not found (or if s is NULL).

   SEE ALSO
	rindex, strchr, index

   (LVO bias -660)

CClib.library/strspn                                      CClib.library/strspn
   NAME
	strspn -- length of initial segment in accept set

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG strspn(STRPTR s, STRPTR accept);

	Registers: a0=s, a1=accept

   FUNCTION
	Measures the length of the initial segment of s consisting only of bytes that appear in the NUL-terminated string accept. Scanning stops at the first byte of s not found in accept, or at the NUL terminator of s.

   INPUTS
	s - string to scan
	accept - NUL-terminated set of bytes to accept

   RESULT
	Number of bytes in the initial accepted segment (0 if s is NULL).

   SEE ALSO
	strcspn, strpbrk

   (LVO bias -666)

CClib.library/strstr                                      CClib.library/strstr
   NAME
	strstr -- find substring in string

   SYNOPSIS
	STRPTR strstr(STRPTR haystack, STRPTR needle);

	Registers: a0=haystack, a1=needle

   FUNCTION
	Searches haystack for the first occurrence of the NUL-terminated substring needle. An empty needle is considered to match at the start of haystack.

   INPUTS
	haystack - string to search in
	needle - substring to find

   RESULT
	Pointer to the first match within haystack, or NULL if needle does not occur (or if haystack is NULL).

   SEE ALSO
	strchr, strcmp, strlen

   (LVO bias -672)

CClib.library/strtok                                      CClib.library/strtok
   NAME
	strtok -- tokenize a string

   SYNOPSIS
	STRPTR strtok(STRPTR s, STRPTR delim);

	Registers: a0=s, a1=delim

   FUNCTION
	Splits a string into tokens separated by any byte from delim. On the first call pass the string in s; on subsequent calls pass NULL to continue from the previous position. Delimiter bytes are overwritten with NUL in the original buffer.

	CClib stores the resume pointer in task_UserData->scanpoint rather than a static C variable, so concurrent tasks do not share strtok state. Only one strtok sequence may be active per task at a time.

   INPUTS
	s - string to tokenize, or NULL to continue
	delim - NUL-terminated set of delimiter bytes

   RESULT
	Pointer to the next token, or NULL when no tokens remain.

   NOTES
	Modifies the string buffer. scanpoint in task_UserData holds the continuation offset between calls.

   SEE ALSO
	strspn, strcspn, strpbrk

   (LVO bias -678)

CClib.library/strrv                                        CClib.library/strrv
   NAME
	strrv -- reverse a string in place

   SYNOPSIS
	STRPTR strrv(STRPTR s);

	Registers: a0=s

   FUNCTION
	Reverses the order of all bytes in the NUL-terminated string s by swapping characters from each end toward the centre. The terminating NUL remains at the end.

   INPUTS
	s - NUL-terminated string to reverse

   RESULT
	s (reversed in place).

   SEE ALSO
	strnrv, strupr

   (LVO bias -684)

CClib.library/strnrv                                      CClib.library/strnrv
   NAME
	strnrv -- reverse first n bytes of a string

   SYNOPSIS
	STRPTR strnrv(STRPTR s, LONG n);

	Registers: a0=s, d0=n

   FUNCTION
	Reverses the first n bytes of s in place. If n exceeds the string length, only the actual string bytes are reversed. Does nothing when n <= 1.

   INPUTS
	s - string to partially reverse
	n - number of leading bytes to reverse

   RESULT
	s.

   SEE ALSO
	strrv

   (LVO bias -690)

CClib.library/bcpl_strcpy                            CClib.library/bcpl_strcpy
   NAME
	bcpl_strcpy -- copy BPTR BCPL string to C string

   SYNOPSIS
	STRPTR bcpl_strcpy(STRPTR dst, BPTR src);

	Registers: a0=dst, a1=src

   FUNCTION
	Copies a BCPL-style string referenced by BPTR src into the C buffer dst. BADDR(src) points at a length byte followed by that many characters (no separate NUL in the BCPL string). A terminating NUL is always written in dst after the copied characters.

	When src is NULL, dst receives an empty C string.

   INPUTS
	dst - destination C buffer
	src - BPTR to BCPL string (length-prefixed)

   RESULT
	dst.

   NOTES
	Used when reading AmigaOS BSTR fields (CLI command names, tool types, etc.).

   SEE ALSO
	strcpy, strncpy

   (LVO bias -696)

CClib.library/vquicksort                              CClib.library/vquicksort
   NAME
	vquicksort -- quicksort by index with compare/swap hooks

   SYNOPSIS
	void vquicksort(ULONG n, APTR cmp, APTR swap);

	Registers: d0=n, a0=cmp, a1=swap

   FUNCTION
	Sorts n abstract elements indexed 0 .. n-1 using caller-supplied compare and swap functions. cmp(a,b) must return negative if element a sorts before b, zero if equal, positive if after. swap(a,b) exchanges the two indexed elements.

	The sort operates on indices only; the caller's hooks map indices to real data (rows in a table, node numbers, etc.). Uses a median-of-three pivot with insertion sort for small partitions.

   INPUTS
	n - number of elements (ULONG)
	cmp - compare hook: LONG (*)(ULONG a, ULONG b)
	swap - swap hook: void (*)(ULONG a, ULONG b)

   RESULT
	None. Elements are reordered via swap.

   SEE ALSO
	quicksort, qsort

   (LVO bias -702)

CClib.library/toupper                                    CClib.library/toupper
   NAME
	toupper -- convert character to uppercase

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG toupper(LONG c);

	Registers: d0=c

   FUNCTION
	Returns the uppercase equivalent of the low byte of c. When utility.library is available, ToUpper is used. Otherwise ASCII 'a'-'z' are mapped to 'A'-'Z'; all other values are returned unchanged.

   INPUTS
	c - character code (LONG, low 8 bits used)

   RESULT
	Uppercase character, or c unchanged if not a lowercase letter.

   SEE ALSO
	tolower, strupr, strcmpa

   (LVO bias -708)

CClib.library/quicksort                                CClib.library/quicksort
   NAME
	quicksort -- quicksort by index with user argument

   SYNOPSIS
	void quicksort(ULONG n, APTR cmp, APTR swap, APTR arg);

	Registers: d0=n, a0=cmp, a1=swap, a2=arg

   FUNCTION
	Like vquicksort but the compare and swap hooks also receive an opaque arg pointer. cmp(a,b,arg) and swap(a,b,arg) implement the same index-based sort with access to caller context (array pointer, sort direction, etc.).

   INPUTS
	n - number of elements (ULONG)
	cmp - compare hook: LONG (*)(ULONG a, ULONG b, APTR arg)
	swap - swap hook: void (*)(ULONG a, ULONG b, APTR arg)
	arg - caller context passed to both hooks

   RESULT
	None.

   SEE ALSO
	vquicksort, qsort

   (LVO bias -714)

CClib.library/qsort                                        CClib.library/qsort
   NAME
	qsort -- sort an array of fixed-size elements

   SYNOPSIS
	void qsort(APTR base, ULONG nel, ULONG width, APTR compar);

	Registers: a0=base, d0=nel, d1=width, a1=compar

   FUNCTION
	Sorts nel elements of width bytes each starting at base. The compar callback receives pointers to two elements and must return a negative LONG if the first sorts before the second, zero if equal, positive if after:

	LONG compar(APTR a, APTR b);

	Internally qsort adapts the abstract quicksort engine with index-based compare/swap wrappers over the byte array.

   INPUTS
	base - pointer to element array
	nel - number of elements (ULONG)
	width - size of each element in bytes (ULONG)
	compar - element compare function

   RESULT
	None. Array contents are reordered in place.

   SEE ALSO
	quicksort, vquicksort

   (LVO bias -720)

CClib.library/cli_parse                                CClib.library/cli_parse
   NAME
	cli_parse -- parse CLI argument vector for SetupSTDIO

   SYNOPSIS
	void cli_parse(APTR ud, LONG alen, STRPTR aptr);

	Registers: a0=ud, d0=alen, a1=aptr

   FUNCTION
	Builds argc/argv in task_UserData from the CLI startup argument block. argv[0] is taken from the CLI command name BSTR when available; remaining tokens are split from aptr (whitespace separated, up to alen bytes). Memory is allocated from the per-task heap via _alloc.

	SetupSTDIO calls cli_parse for CLI-launched programs so main(argc,argv) wrappers see a conventional argv.

   INPUTS
	ud - task_UserData pointer
	alen - length of CLI argument bytes
	aptr - pointer to CLI argument string (may be NULL)

   RESULT
	None. Sets ud->_argc, ud->_argv, ud->_arg_lin.

   NOTES
	Called from SetupSTDIO; not usually invoked by applications directly.

   SEE ALSO
	wb_parse, SetupSTDIO

   (LVO bias -726)

CClib.library/wb_parse                                  CClib.library/wb_parse
   NAME
	wb_parse -- parse Workbench startup for SetupSTDIO

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG wb_parse(APTR ud, APTR wbm);

	Registers: a0=ud, a1=wbm

   FUNCTION
	Processes a WBStartup message for a Workbench-launched program. Builds argc/argv from sm_ArgList (argv[0] is the tool file name). Stores the WBStartup pointer in ud->wbmsg.

	If the tool icon has a WINDOW tooltype, opens that path and SelectInput/SelectOutput so stdio is attached to the named window file. Without WINDOW, SetupSTDIO falls back to NIL: handles and stdio may be unavailable under Workbench.

   INPUTS
	ud - task_UserData pointer
	wbm - pointer to struct WBStartup message

   RESULT
	1 if parsing succeeded (and WINDOW opened when present), 0 on failure.

   NOTES
	Workbench programs need WINDOW in the icon tooltypes for console I/O. icon.library is opened temporarily.

   SEE ALSO
	cli_parse, SetupSTDIO, wb_exec

   (LVO bias -732)

CClib.library/vprintf                                    CClib.library/vprintf
   NAME
	vprintf -- formatted output to stdout via arg array

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG vprintf(STRPTR format, APTR args);

	Registers: a0=format, a1=args

   FUNCTION
	Formats output according to format and writes to the task's stdout FILE. args is an APTR array of arguments in RawDoFmt order (not a C varargs stack). Integer and string conversions use one array slot; %e/%f/%g consume a DOUBLE as two consecutive LONG slots.

	This is the LVO behind printf when linked through the stub layer.

   INPUTS
	format - printf-style format string
	args - APTR array of format arguments

   RESULT
	Number of characters written, or -1 on error (no stdout or format failure).

   NOTES
	LVO takes (format, args) only. Client stubs build the args array from C varargs.

   SEE ALSO
	printf, vfprintf, vsprintf

   (LVO bias -738)

CClib.library/vfprintf                                  CClib.library/vfprintf
   NAME
	vfprintf -- formatted output to a stream via arg array

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG vfprintf(APTR stream, STRPTR format, APTR args);

	Registers: a0=stream, a1=format, a2=args

   FUNCTION
	Like vprintf but writes to the given FILE stream. Uses the same RawDoFmt-style args array as the other printf LVOs.

   INPUTS
	stream - FILE pointer (APTR at LVO boundary)
	format - printf-style format string
	args - APTR array of format arguments

   RESULT
	Number of characters written, or -1 on error.

   SEE ALSO
	fprintf, vprintf, vsprintf

   (LVO bias -744)

CClib.library/vsprintf                                  CClib.library/vsprintf
   NAME
	vsprintf -- formatted output to a string via arg array

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG vsprintf(STRPTR s, STRPTR format, APTR args);

	Registers: a0=s, a1=format, a2=args

   FUNCTION
	Formats into the buffer s according to format using the RawDoFmt args array. A terminating NUL is always written. The buffer must be large enough for the expanded result.

   INPUTS
	s - destination character buffer
	format - printf-style format string
	args - APTR array of format arguments

   RESULT
	Number of characters written (excluding NUL), or -1 on error.

   SEE ALSO
	sprintf, vprintf, vfprintf

   (LVO bias -750)

CClib.library/fgetpos                                    CClib.library/fgetpos
   NAME
	fgetpos -- read current stream position

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG fgetpos(APTR stream, APTR pos);

	Registers: a0=stream, a1=pos

   FUNCTION
	Stores the current read/write offset of stream into the LONG pointed to by pos. Accounts for stdio buffering by adjusting the underlying file position returned by ftell.

   INPUTS
	stream - FILE pointer
	pos - pointer to LONG to receive position

   RESULT
	0 on success, -1 on error (sets errno).

   SEE ALSO
	fsetpos, ftell, fseek

   (LVO bias -756)

CClib.library/fsetpos                                    CClib.library/fsetpos
   NAME
	fsetpos -- restore stream position

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG fsetpos(APTR stream, APTR pos);

	Registers: a0=stream, a1=pos

   FUNCTION
	Sets the read/write position of stream to the value previously stored by fgetpos. Implemented as fseek(stream, *pos, SEEK_SET).

   INPUTS
	stream - FILE pointer
	pos - pointer to LONG position from fgetpos

   RESULT
	0 on success, -1 on error (sets errno).

   SEE ALSO
	fgetpos, fseek, ftell

   (LVO bias -762)

CClib.library/time                                          CClib.library/time
   NAME
	time -- seconds since Amiga epoch

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG time(APTR tloc);

	Registers: a0=tloc

   FUNCTION
	Returns the current time as seconds since 1978-01-01 00:00:00 (Amiga epoch), derived from DateStamp via utility.library conversion. When tloc is non-NULL, the same value is stored at *tloc.

   INPUTS
	tloc - optional pointer to LONG to receive time, or NULL

   RESULT
	Current time in seconds (Amiga epoch).

   NOTES
	Amiga epoch is 1978-01-01, not Unix 1970. Offset is roughly eight years.

   SEE ALSO
	clock, gmtime, localtime, mktime

   (LVO bias -768)

CClib.library/clock                                        CClib.library/clock
   NAME
	clock -- elapsed processor time for this task

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG clock(void);

   FUNCTION
	Returns an approximation of elapsed time for the current task since SetupSTDIO recorded ud->start. Uses the same Amiga-epoch seconds as time() minus the startup stamp.

   INPUTS
	None.

   RESULT
	Elapsed seconds since task stdio setup, or current time if task_UserData is unavailable.

   SEE ALSO
	time, difftime

   (LVO bias -774)

CClib.library/gmtime                                      CClib.library/gmtime
   NAME
	gmtime -- break seconds into struct tm

   SYNOPSIS
	APTR gmtime(APTR timer);

	Registers: a0=timer

   FUNCTION
	Converts the Amiga-epoch seconds pointed to by timer into a struct tm stored in task_UserData->tm. Fields follow ANSI conventions (tm_mon 0-11, tm_year years since 1900, etc.), filled via Amiga2Date.

   INPUTS
	timer - pointer to LONG seconds since Amiga epoch

   RESULT
	Pointer to ud->tm, or NULL if task_UserData or timer is NULL.

   NOTES
	Returned struct tm is per-task storage; not safe across nested calls in the same task.

   SEE ALSO
	localtime, mktime, asctime, ctime

   (LVO bias -780)

CClib.library/asctime                                    CClib.library/asctime
   NAME
	asctime -- format struct tm as text

   SYNOPSIS
	STRPTR asctime(APTR tm);

	Registers: a0=tm

   FUNCTION
	Formats the struct tm pointed to by tm into a fixed-width ASCII string of the form:

	Sun Jan  1 12:00:00 1989\n

	The result is built into task_UserData->buffer via sprintf.

   INPUTS
	tm - pointer to struct tm to format

   RESULT
	Pointer to ud->buffer, or NULL on error. Buffer is overwritten on each call.

   NOTES
	Per-task static buffer in task_UserData; not re-entrant within one task.

   SEE ALSO
	ctime, gmtime, localtime, strftime

   (LVO bias -786)

CClib.library/ctime                                        CClib.library/ctime
   NAME
	ctime -- convert time value to ASCII string

   SYNOPSIS
	STRPTR ctime(APTR timer);

	Registers: a0=timer

   FUNCTION
	Converts the Amiga-epoch seconds pointed to by timer to a struct tm via localtime, then formats with asctime. Convenience wrapper equivalent to asctime(localtime(timer)).

   INPUTS
	timer - pointer to LONG seconds since Amiga epoch

   RESULT
	Pointer to formatted string in task_UserData->buffer, or NULL.

   SEE ALSO
	asctime, localtime, time

   (LVO bias -792)

CClib.library/localtime                                CClib.library/localtime
   NAME
	localtime -- convert seconds to struct tm (no timezone)

   SYNOPSIS
	APTR localtime(APTR timer);

	Registers: a0=timer

   FUNCTION
	Identical to gmtime. AmigaOS has no timezone database in CClib, so local time and UTC are the same conversion.

   INPUTS
	timer - pointer to LONG seconds since Amiga epoch

   RESULT
	Pointer to ud->tm, or NULL on error.

   NOTES
	gmtime and localtime return the same per-task struct tm.

   SEE ALSO
	gmtime, mktime, ctime

   (LVO bias -798)

CClib.library/mktime                                      CClib.library/mktime
   NAME
	mktime -- convert struct tm to seconds

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG mktime(APTR tm);

	Registers: a0=tm

   FUNCTION
	Normalizes the struct tm pointed to by tm and returns Amiga-epoch seconds. Uses Date2Amiga when month and day are valid; otherwise derives seconds from tm_year and tm_yday.

   INPUTS
	tm - pointer to struct tm (fields may be updated)

   RESULT
	Seconds since Amiga epoch, or -1 if tm is NULL or conversion fails.

   SEE ALSO
	gmtime, localtime, time, difftime

   (LVO bias -804)

CClib.library/difftime                                  CClib.library/difftime
   NAME
	difftime -- difference between two time values

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG difftime(LONG time1, LONG time0);

	Registers: d0=time1, d1=time0

   FUNCTION
	Returns time1 minus time0 as a signed LONG. Both values are expected to be Amiga-epoch seconds from time() or mktime().

   INPUTS
	time1 - later time value
	time0 - earlier time value

   RESULT
	time1 - time0 in seconds.

   SEE ALSO
	time, mktime, clock

   (LVO bias -810)

CClib.library/strftime                                  CClib.library/strftime
   NAME
	strftime -- format struct tm into a string

   SYNOPSIS
	ULONG strftime(STRPTR s, ULONG maxsize, STRPTR format, APTR tm);

	Registers: a0=s, d0=maxsize, a1=format, a2=tm

   FUNCTION
	Formats the struct tm pointed to by tm into s according to format, writing at most maxsize bytes including a terminating NUL. Supports common conversion specifiers (%a, %A, %b, %B, %c, %d, %H, %I, %j, %m, %M, %p, %S, %U, %w, %W, %x, %X, %y, %Y, %% and others as implemented).

   INPUTS
	s - output buffer
	maxsize - size of s including room for NUL
	format - strftime format string
	tm - struct tm to format

   RESULT
	Number of bytes placed in s (excluding NUL), or 0 if the result does not fit in maxsize.

   SEE ALSO
	asctime, gmtime, localtime

   (LVO bias -816)

CClib.library/scdir                                        CClib.library/scdir
   NAME
	scdir -- enumerate files matching a pattern

   SYNOPSIS
	STRPTR scdir(STRPTR pattern);

	Registers: a0=pattern

   FUNCTION
	Directory scanner built on MatchFirst/MatchNext. On the first call with a pattern, converts shell-style * wildcards to Amiga '#?' and starts a scan stored in task_UserData->scdir_mem. Returns a pointer to the current entry file name.

	Subsequent calls with any pattern (usually the same) advance with MatchNext until no more matches, then end the scan and return NULL. Call scdir_clean to abort mid-scan.

	Do not pass patterns that reference non-existent volume names; AmigaDOS may display a volume requester. Use existing volumes (e.g. T:, LIBS:) or specific file paths instead.

   INPUTS
	pattern - file pattern with optional * and ? wildcards (required on first call of a sequence)

   RESULT
	Pointer to current file name, or NULL when the scan ends or fails. errno may be set on allocation failure.

   NOTES
	Scan state is per-task in scdir_mem. Pattern is ignored after the first call until the scan completes or scdir_clean runs.

   SEE ALSO
	scdir_clean, getenv, stat_

   (LVO bias -822)

CClib.library/scdir_clean                            CClib.library/scdir_clean
   NAME
	scdir_clean -- abort an scdir scan

   SYNOPSIS
	void scdir_clean(void);

   FUNCTION
	Ends an in-progress scdir scan: calls MatchEnd, frees the AnchorPath in task_UserData->scdir_mem, and clears the pointer. Safe to call when no scan is active.

   INPUTS
	None.

   RESULT
	None.

   NOTES
	Call before starting a new pattern or when abandoning a partial enumeration.

   SEE ALSO
	scdir

   (LVO bias -828)

CClib.library/tolower                                    CClib.library/tolower
   NAME
	tolower -- convert character to lowercase

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG tolower(LONG c);

	Registers: d0=c

   FUNCTION
	Returns the lowercase equivalent of the low byte of c. When utility.library is available, ToLower is used. Otherwise ASCII 'A'-'Z' map to 'a'-'z'; other values are unchanged.

   INPUTS
	c - character code (LONG, low 8 bits used)

   RESULT
	Lowercase character, or c unchanged if not uppercase.

   SEE ALSO
	toupper, strupr, strcmpa

   (LVO bias -834)

CClib.library/setenv                                      CClib.library/setenv
   NAME
	setenv -- set or delete an environment variable

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG setenv(STRPTR name, STRPTR value);

	Registers: a0=name, a1=value

   FUNCTION
	Wraps dos.library SetVar / DeleteVar. Sets a process-local
	variable (GVF_LOCAL_ONLY). When value is NULL or "", deletes
	the local var, then any global ENV: copy (Amiga SETENV empty
	semantics).

   INPUTS
	name - variable name
	value - new value string, or NULL/"" to delete

   RESULT
	0 on success, -1 on failure (errno set to EINVAL, ENOENT, or ENOMEM).

   NOTES
	Prefers local vars (SetVar default). See also dos.library/SetVar.

   SEE ALSO
	getenv, unsetenv

   (LVO bias -840)

CClib.library/getenv                                      CClib.library/getenv
   NAME
	getenv -- read an environment variable

   SYNOPSIS
	STRPTR getenv(STRPTR name);

	Registers: a0=name

   FUNCTION
	Wraps dos.library GetVar with flags 0 (local process var first,
	then global ENV:). Copies the value into a per-task scratch
	buffer.

   INPUTS
	name - variable name to look up

   RESULT
	Pointer to the value string, or NULL if not found. The buffer is freed on the next getenv call in the same task.

   NOTES
	Return pointer is valid only until the next getenv in this task. Copy the string if it must be retained.
	See also dos.library/GetVar.

   SEE ALSO
	setenv, unsetenv

   (LVO bias -846)

CClib.library/unsetenv                                  CClib.library/unsetenv
   NAME
	unsetenv -- delete an environment variable

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG unsetenv(STRPTR name);

	Registers: a0=name

   FUNCTION
	Wraps dos.library DeleteVar. Removes the local var, then any
	global ENV: copy so GetVar's default lookup cannot see it.
	Missing name is success (POSIX).

   RESULT
	0 on success, -1 if name is NULL or empty (errno EINVAL).

   SEE ALSO
	setenv, getenv

   (LVO bias -966)

CClib.library/stat_                                        CClib.library/stat_
   NAME
	stat_ -- extended file status

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG stat_(STRPTR name, APTR buf);

	Registers: a0=name, a1=buf

   FUNCTION
	Fills the cc_stat buffer for the named file or directory. Like stat but also sets st_mode (file/directory), st_nlink (always 1), st_atime, and st_ctime from the FileInfoBlock. st_mtime and st_size are always filled; st_attr holds Amiga protection bits.

   INPUTS
	name - path to examine
	buf - pointer to cc_stat structure to fill

   RESULT
	0 on success, -1 on failure (errno ENOENT, ENOMEM, EACCES, or EINVAL).

   NOTES
	stat() is the classic subset (mode/nlink/atime/ctime zeroed). Use stat_ when full POSIX-like fields are needed.

   SEE ALSO
	stat, access, scdir

   (LVO bias -852)

CClib.library/wb_exec                                    CClib.library/wb_exec
   NAME
	wb_exec -- launch a Workbench child process

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG wb_exec(STRPTR filename, STRPTR procname, STRPTR toolname,
	STRPTR toolwindow, BPTR curdir, APTR rport, LONG stk, LONG pri,
	APTR child);

	Registers: a0=filename, a1=procname, a2=toolname, a3=toolwindow,
		a4=curdir, a5=rport, d0=stk, d1=pri, d2=child

   FUNCTION
	Loads filename as an Amiga executable segment, creates a new process, and sends a WBStartup message to it. The child structure (cc_Child) must be supplied by the caller; segment, locks, and reply port are filled in. Does not wait for exit.

	Searches the current directory then C: for the file. curdir selects the initial lock passed in wa_Lock; rport receives the WBStartup reply (defaults to the per-task exeport).

   INPUTS
	filename - executable file name
	procname - process name tag (or filename)
	toolname - WBStartup tool name (wa_Name)
	toolwindow - WBStartup window string
	curdir - BPTR lock for initial directory, or -1 for parent's
	rport - reply MsgPort, or NULL for default
	stk - stack size (0 = parent default)
	pri - process priority
	child - pointer to cc_Child to initialize

   RESULT
	1 if launch succeeded, 0 on failure.

   SEE ALSO
	wbexec, wbAexec, wait_child, exec

   (LVO bias -858)

CClib.library/wbexec                                      CClib.library/wbexec
   NAME
	wbexec -- run a program from Workbench and wait

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG wbexec(STRPTR name, LONG stk, LONG pri);

	Registers: a0=name, d0=stk, d1=pri

   FUNCTION
	Convenience wrapper: allocates a stack-local cc_Child, calls wb_exec with default tool/window fields, then wait_child to block until the child exits. Synchronous Workbench launch from the parent's context.

   INPUTS
	name - executable file name
	stk - stack size (0 = default)
	pri - process priority

   RESULT
	1 if launch and wait succeeded, 0 on failure.

   SEE ALSO
	wb_exec, wbAexec, exec, wait_child

   (LVO bias -864)

CClib.library/wbAexec                                    CClib.library/wbAexec
   NAME
	wbAexec -- launch Workbench child asynchronously

   SYNOPSIS
	APTR wbAexec(STRPTR name, LONG stk, LONG pri);

	Registers: a0=name, d0=stk, d1=pri

   FUNCTION
	Like wbexec but allocates a cc_Child on the task's Children list and returns immediately without waiting. The returned Child pointer is used with wait_child, kill, or signal_child.

   INPUTS
	name - executable file name
	stk - stack size (0 = default)
	pri - process priority

   RESULT
	Pointer to cc_Child on success, or NULL on failure (errno may be ENOMEM).

   NOTES
	Child remains on ud->Children until wait_child or kill reaps it.

   SEE ALSO
	wb_exec, wbexec, wait_child, kill

   (LVO bias -870)

CClib.library/wait_child                              CClib.library/wait_child
   NAME
	wait_child -- wait for one child to exit

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG wait_child(APTR child);

	Registers: a0=child

   FUNCTION
	Blocks until the given cc_Child completes. Waits on the per-task exeport for the WBStartup return message, then unloads the segment, releases locks, and removes the child from the Children list when linked.

	When child is NULL, waits for all children (same as wait_children).

   INPUTS
	child - cc_Child pointer from wbAexec, or NULL for all

   RESULT
	1 when the child has exited, 0 on error.

   NOTES
	Also services DOS break signals via the abort handler while waiting.

   SEE ALSO
	wait_children, wbAexec, kill

   (LVO bias -876)

CClib.library/wait_children                        CClib.library/wait_children
   NAME
	wait_children -- wait for all async children

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG wait_children(void);

   FUNCTION
	Repeatedly waits until every cc_Child on task_UserData->Children has returned its WBStartup message and been reaped.

   INPUTS
	None.

   RESULT
	1 when all children have exited, 0 on error.

   SEE ALSO
	wait_child, wbAexec, kill_children

   (LVO bias -882)

CClib.library/signal_child                          CClib.library/signal_child
   NAME
	signal_child -- send an Exec signal to a child task

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG signal_child(APTR child, ULONG sig);

	Registers: a0=child, d0=sig

   FUNCTION
	Calls Signal() on the child process task with the given signal mask. Used to notify an async child without terminating it.

   INPUTS
	child - cc_Child pointer
	sig - signal mask (ULONG)

   RESULT
	1 if signalled, 0 if child or pid is NULL.

   SEE ALSO
	kill, wait_child, wbAexec

   (LVO bias -888)

CClib.library/kill                                          CClib.library/kill
   NAME
	kill -- break signal and wait for one child

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG kill(APTR child);

	Registers: a0=child

   FUNCTION
	Sends the DOS break signal mask (Ctrl-C/D/E/F) to the child process, then waits for it to exit via wait_child. This is not the Unix kill(2) syscall; no signal number is supplied beyond the fixed break mask.

   INPUTS
	child - cc_Child pointer to terminate

   RESULT
	Result of wait_child after signalling.

   NOTES
	Sends SIGBREAKF_CTRL_* bits, not arbitrary Unix signals.

   SEE ALSO
	kill_children, signal_child, wait_child

   (LVO bias -894)

CClib.library/kill_children                        CClib.library/kill_children
   NAME
	kill_children -- break signal and wait for all children

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG kill_children(void);

   FUNCTION
	Sends the DOS break mask to every child on the Children list, then calls wait_children to reap them all.

   INPUTS
	None.

   RESULT
	Result of wait_children.

   SEE ALSO
	kill, wait_children, wbAexec

   (LVO bias -900)

CClib.library/cliexec                                    CClib.library/cliexec
   NAME
	cliexec -- run a CLI command via Execute

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG cliexec(STRPTR cmd);

	Registers: a0=cmd

   FUNCTION
	Runs cmd through the AmigaDOS Execute() function using the current CLI process Input() and Output() handles. Only works when the caller is a CLI process (pr_CLI non-NULL).

   INPUTS
	cmd - command string to execute

   RESULT
	1 if Execute succeeded, 0 if not CLI or cmd is NULL.

   NOTES
	Does not wait for completion beyond Execute's own behaviour. Use exec or SystemTags for shell commands that must finish before returning.

   SEE ALSO
	exec, cli_parse

   (LVO bias -906)

CClib.library/exec                                          CClib.library/exec
   NAME
	exec -- run a command and wait for completion

   SYNOPSIS
	LONG exec(STRPTR cmd);

	Registers: a0=cmd

   FUNCTION
	Runs cmd and blocks until it finishes. Behaviour depends on the parent process:

	CLI parent: runs cmd via SystemTags with the shell Input/Output handles and waits for the system command to complete.

	Workbench parent: extracts the first whitespace-delimited token as a file name and calls wbexec synchronously.

   INPUTS
	cmd - command line or program name

   RESULT
	1 on success, 0 on failure.

   NOTES
	CLI path uses SystemTags; WB path uses wbexec. Not a POSIX execve replacement.

   SEE ALSO
	cliexec, wbexec, wb_exec

   (LVO bias -912)
